window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-06-3", "source": "2015年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on the saying“ If you cannot do great things, do small things in a great way.\"You can cite examples to illustrate your point of view. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The saying \"If you cannot do great things, do small things in a great way\" reminds us that true value lies not in\nthe scale of what we do, but in the devotion and care we put into it. In my view, greatness is an attitude rather\nthan a matter of size.\nConsider an ordinary street cleaner who sweeps every corner spotless before dawn, or a rural teacher who\nspends decades patiently guiding children in a remote village. Neither builds an empire, yet by pouring\nperfection into humble tasks, they make an impact far beyond their modest positions. Their example shows that\neven the smallest job, done with heart, can shine. Had they despised such work as trivial, their potential would\nhave been wasted.\nTherefore, we should never look down on small things or wait passively for great opportunities. Even if we\ncannot change the world overnight, doing each ordinary task in an extraordinary way is itself a form of\ngreatness worth pursuing."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国传统的待客之道要求饭菜丰富多样，让客人吃不完。中国宴席上典型的菜单包括开席的一套凉菜及其后的热菜，例如肉类、鸡鸭、蔬菜等。大多数宴席上，全鱼被认为是必不可少的，除非已经上过各式海鲜。如今，中国人喜欢把西方特色菜与传统中式菜肴融于一席，因此牛排上桌也不少见。沙拉也已流行起来，尽管传统上中国人一般不吃任何未经烹饪的菜肴。宴席通常至少有一道汤，可以最先或最后上桌。甜点和水果通常标志宴席的结束。", "sample": "The traditional Chinese way of hosting guests requires a rich variety of food, so that the guests cannot finish all the dishes. A typical menu at a Chinese banquet includes a set of cold dishes to start the meal, followed by hot dishes such as meat, chicken, duck and vegetables. At most banquets, a whole fish is considered essential, unless various kinds of seafood have already been served. Nowadays, Chinese people like to combine Western specialties with traditional Chinese dishes at one banquet, so it is not uncommon to see steak on the table. Salads have also become popular, although traditionally Chinese people seldom eat any uncooked dishes. There is usually at least one soup at a banquet, which can be served either first or last. Desserts and fruit usually mark the end of the banquet."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-06-3/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Travel websites have been around since the 1990s, when Expedia, Travelocity, and other holiday booking sites were launched, allowing travelers to compare flight and hotel prices with the click of a mouse. With information no longer  __26__  by travel agents or hidden in business networks, the travel industry was revolutionized, as greater transparency helped  __27__  prices.\nToday, the industry is going through a new revolution—this time transforming service quality. Online rating platforms—  __28__  in hotels, restaurants, apartments, and taxis—allow travelers to exchange reviews and experiences for all to see.\nHospitality businesses are now ranked, analyzed, and compared not by industry  __29__  , but by the very people for whom the service is intended—the customer. This has  __30__  a new relationship between buyer and seller. Customers have always voted with their feet; they can now explain their decision to anyone who is interested. As a result, businesses are much more  __31__  , often in very specific ways, which creates powerful  __32__  to improve service.\nAlthough some readers might not care for gossipy reports of unfriendly bellboys(行李员) in Berlin or malfunctioning hotel hairdryers in Houston, the true power of online reviews lies not just in the individual stories, but in the websites'  __33__  to aggregate a large volume of ratings.\nThe impact cannot be  __34__  . Businesses that attract top ratings can enjoy rapid growth, as new customers are attracted by good reviews and  __35__  provide yet more positive feedback. So great is the influence of online ratings that many companies now hire digital reputation managers to ensure a favorable online identity.", "matchIntro": "Plastic Surgery\nA better credit card is the solution to ever larger hack attacks.", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "A thin magnetic stripe (magstripe) is all that stands between your credit-card information and the bad guys. And they've been working hard to break in. That's why 2014 is shaping up as a major showdown: banks, law enforcement and technology companies are all trying to stop a network of hackers who are succeeding in stealing account numbers, names, email addresses and other crucial data used in identity theft. More than 100 million accounts at Target, Neiman Marcus and Michaels stores were affected in some way during the most recent attacks, starting last November."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Swipe(刷卡) is the operative word: cards are increasingly vulnerable to attacks when you make purchases in a store. In several recent incidents, hackers have been able to obtain massive information of credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-card numbers using malware, i.e. malicious software, inserted secretly into the retailers' point-of-sale system—the checkout registers. Hackers then sold the data to a second group of criminals operating in shadowy corners of the web. Not long after, the stolen data was showing up on fake cards and being used for online purchases."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The solution could cost as little as $2 extra for every piece of plastic issued. The fix is a security technology used heavily outside the U.S. While American credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to process transactions, much of the rest of the world uses smarter cards with a technology called EMV (short for Europay, MasterCard, Visa) that employs a chip embedded in the card plus a customer PIN (personal identification number) to authenticate(验证) every transaction on the spot. If a purchaser fails to punch in the correct PIN at the checkout, the transaction gets rejected. (Online purchases can be made by setting up a separate transaction code.)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Why haven't big banks adopted the more secure technology? When it comes to mailing out new credit cards, it's all about relative costs, says David Robertson, who runs the Nilson Report, an industry newsletter. \"The cost of the card, putting the sticker on it, coding the account number and expiration date, embossing(凸印) it, the small envelope—all put together, you're in the dollar range.\" A chip-and-PIN card currently costs closer to $3, says Robertson, because of the price of chips. (Once large issuers convert together, the chip costs should drop.)"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Multiply $3 by the more than 5 billion magstripe credit and prepaid cards in circulation in the U.S. Then consider that there's an estimated $12.4 billion in card fraud on a global basis, says Robertson. With 44% of that in the U.S., American credit-card fraud amounts to about $5.5 billion annually. Card issuers have so far calculated that absorbing the liability for even big hacks like the Target one is still cheaper than replacing all that plastic."}, {"label": "F", "text": "That leaves American retailers pretty much alone the world over in relying on magstripe technology to charge purchases—and leaves consumers vulnerable. Each magstripe has three tracks of information, explains payments security expert Jeremy Gumbley, the chief technology officer of Credit Call, an electronic-payments company. The first and third are used by the bank or card issuer. Your vital account information lives on the second track, which hackers try to capture. \"Malware is scanning through the memory in real time and looking for data,\" he says. \"It creates a text file that gets stolen.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Chip-and-PIN cards, by contrast, make fake cards or skimming impossible because the information that gets scanned is encrypted(加密). The historical reason the U.S. has stuck with magstripe, ironically enough, is once superior technology. Our cheap, ultra-reliable wired networks made credit-card authentication over the phone frictionless. In France, card companies created EMV in part because the telephone monopoly was so maddeningly inefficient and expensive. The EMV solution allowed transactions to be verified locally and securely."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Some big banks, like Wells Fargo, are now offering to convert your magstripe card to a chip-and-PIN model. It's actually a hybrid(混合体) that will still have a magstripe, since most U.S. merchants don't have EMV terminals. Should you take them up on it? If you travel internationally, the answer is yes."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Keep in mind, too, that credit cards typically have better liability protection than debit cards. If someone uses your credit card fraudulently(欺诈性的), it's the issuer or merchant, not you, that takes the hit. Debit cards have different liability limits depending on the bank and the events surrounding any fraud. \"If it's available, the logical thing is to get a chip-and-PIN card from your bank,\" says Eric Adamowsky, a co-founder of Credit CardInsider.com. \"I would use credit cards over debit cards because of liability issues.\" Cash still works pretty well too."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Retailers and banks stand to benefit from the lower fraud levels of chip-and-PIN cards but have been reluctant for years to invest in the new infrastructure(基础设施) needed for the technology, especially if consumers don't have access to it. It's a chicken-and-egg problem: no one wants to spend the money on upgraded point-of-sale systems that can read the chip cards if shoppers aren't carrying them—yet there's little point in consumers' carrying the fancy plastic if stores aren't equipped to use them. (An earlier effort by Target to move to chip and PIN never gained progress.) According to Gumbley, there's a \"you-first mentality. The logjam(僵局) has to be broken.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon recently expressed his willingness to do so, noting that banks and merchants have spent the past decade suing each other over interchange fees—the percentage of the transaction price they keep—rather than deal with the growing hacking problem. Chase offers a chip-enabled card under its own brand and several others for travel-related companies such as British Airways and Ritz-Carlton."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The Target and Neiman hacks have also changed the cost calculation: although retailers have been reluctant to spend the $6.75 billion that Capgemini consultants estimate it will take to convert all their registers to be chip-and-PIN-compatible, the potential liability they now face is dramatically greater. Target has been hit with class actions from hacked consumers. \"It's the ultimate nightmare,\" a retail executive from a well-known chain admitted to TIME."}, {"label": "M", "text": "The card-payment companies MasterCard and Visa are pushing hard for change. The two firms have warned all parties in the transaction chain—merchant, network, bank—that if they don't become EMV-compliant by October 2015, the party that is least compliant will bear the fraud risk."}, {"label": "N", "text": "In the meantime, app-equipped smartphones and digital wallets—all of which can use EMV technology—are beginning to make inroads(侵染) on cards and cash. PayPal, for instance, is testing an app that lets you use your mobile phone to pay on the fly at local merchants—without surrendering any card information to them. And further down the road is biometric authentication, which could be encrypted with, say, a fingerprint."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Credit and debit cards, though, are going to be with us for the foreseeable future, and so are hackers, if we stick with magstripe technology. \"It seems crazy to me,\" says Gumbley, who is English, \"that a cutting-edge technology country is depending on a 40-year-old technology.\" That's why it may be up to consumers to move the needle on chip and PIN. Says Robertson: \"When you get the consumer into a position of worry and inconvenience, that's where the rubber hits the road.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "What's the one word of advice a well-meaning professional would give to a recent college graduate? China? India? Brazil? How about trade?\nWhen the Commerce Department reported last week that the trade deficit in June approached $50 billion, it set off a new round of economic doomsaying. Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion in the month, are subtracted in the calculation of gross domestic product. The larger the trade deficit, the smaller the GDP. Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\nBut there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on imports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the broad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\nThe rising volume of trade—more goods and services shuttling in and out of the United States—is good news for many sectors. Companies engaged in shipping, trucking, rail freight, delivery, and logistics(物流) have all been reporting better than expected results. The rising numbers signify growing vitality in foreign markets—when we import more stuff, it puts more cash in the hands of people around the world, and U.S. exports are rising because more foreigners have the ability to buy the things we produce and market. The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses, especially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\nAnd while exports always seem to lag, U.S. companies are becoming more involved in the global economy with each passing month. General Motors sells as many cars in China as in America each month. While that may not do much for imports, it does help GM's balance sheet—and hence makes the jobs of U.S.-based executives more stable.\nOne great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. That's to be expected, given what we've been through. But there's a bigger challenge. Can U.S.-based businesses, large and small, figure out how to get a piece of growing global demand? Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China, the best way to do that is through trade. It may seem obvious, but it's no longer enough simply to do business with our friends and neighbors here at home.\nCompanies and individuals who don't have a strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global trade, are shutting themselves out of the lion's share of economic opportunity in our world.", "47": "What's the one word of advice a well-meaning professional would give to a recent college graduate? China? India? Brazil? How about trade?\nWhen the Commerce Department reported last week that the trade deficit in June approached $50 billion, it set off a new round of economic doomsaying. Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion in the month, are subtracted in the calculation of gross domestic product. The larger the trade deficit, the smaller the GDP. Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\nBut there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on imports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the broad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\nThe rising volume of trade—more goods and services shuttling in and out of the United States—is good news for many sectors. Companies engaged in shipping, trucking, rail freight, delivery, and logistics(物流) have all been reporting better than expected results. The rising numbers signify growing vitality in foreign markets—when we import more stuff, it puts more cash in the hands of people around the world, and U.S. exports are rising because more foreigners have the ability to buy the things we produce and market. The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses, especially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\nAnd while exports always seem to lag, U.S. companies are becoming more involved in the global economy with each passing month. General Motors sells as many cars in China as in America each month. While that may not do much for imports, it does help GM's balance sheet—and hence makes the jobs of U.S.-based executives more stable.\nOne great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. That's to be expected, given what we've been through. But there's a bigger challenge. Can U.S.-based businesses, large and small, figure out how to get a piece of growing global demand? Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China, the best way to do that is through trade. It may seem obvious, but it's no longer enough simply to do business with our friends and neighbors here at home.\nCompanies and individuals who don't have a strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global trade, are shutting themselves out of the lion's share of economic opportunity in our world.", "48": "What's the one word of advice a well-meaning professional would give to a recent college graduate? China? India? Brazil? How about trade?\nWhen the Commerce Department reported last week that the trade deficit in June approached $50 billion, it set off a new round of economic doomsaying. Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion in the month, are subtracted in the calculation of gross domestic product. The larger the trade deficit, the smaller the GDP. Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\nBut there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on imports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the broad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\nThe rising volume of trade—more goods and services shuttling in and out of the United States—is good news for many sectors. Companies engaged in shipping, trucking, rail freight, delivery, and logistics(物流) have all been reporting better than expected results. The rising numbers signify growing vitality in foreign markets—when we import more stuff, it puts more cash in the hands of people around the world, and U.S. exports are rising because more foreigners have the ability to buy the things we produce and market. The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses, especially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\nAnd while exports always seem to lag, U.S. companies are becoming more involved in the global economy with each passing month. General Motors sells as many cars in China as in America each month. While that may not do much for imports, it does help GM's balance sheet—and hence makes the jobs of U.S.-based executives more stable.\nOne great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. That's to be expected, given what we've been through. But there's a bigger challenge. Can U.S.-based businesses, large and small, figure out how to get a piece of growing global demand? Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China, the best way to do that is through trade. It may seem obvious, but it's no longer enough simply to do business with our friends and neighbors here at home.\nCompanies and individuals who don't have a strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global trade, are shutting themselves out of the lion's share of economic opportunity in our world.", "49": "What's the one word of advice a well-meaning professional would give to a recent college graduate? China? India? Brazil? How about trade?\nWhen the Commerce Department reported last week that the trade deficit in June approached $50 billion, it set off a new round of economic doomsaying. Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion in the month, are subtracted in the calculation of gross domestic product. The larger the trade deficit, the smaller the GDP. Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\nBut there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on imports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the broad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\nThe rising volume of trade—more goods and services shuttling in and out of the United States—is good news for many sectors. Companies engaged in shipping, trucking, rail freight, delivery, and logistics(物流) have all been reporting better than expected results. The rising numbers signify growing vitality in foreign markets—when we import more stuff, it puts more cash in the hands of people around the world, and U.S. exports are rising because more foreigners have the ability to buy the things we produce and market. The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses, especially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\nAnd while exports always seem to lag, U.S. companies are becoming more involved in the global economy with each passing month. General Motors sells as many cars in China as in America each month. While that may not do much for imports, it does help GM's balance sheet—and hence makes the jobs of U.S.-based executives more stable.\nOne great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. That's to be expected, given what we've been through. But there's a bigger challenge. Can U.S.-based businesses, large and small, figure out how to get a piece of growing global demand? Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China, the best way to do that is through trade. It may seem obvious, but it's no longer enough simply to do business with our friends and neighbors here at home.\nCompanies and individuals who don't have a strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global trade, are shutting themselves out of the lion's share of economic opportunity in our world.", "50": "What's the one word of advice a well-meaning professional would give to a recent college graduate? China? India? Brazil? How about trade?\nWhen the Commerce Department reported last week that the trade deficit in June approached $50 billion, it set off a new round of economic doomsaying. Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion in the month, are subtracted in the calculation of gross domestic product. The larger the trade deficit, the smaller the GDP. Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\nBut there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on imports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the broad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\nThe rising volume of trade—more goods and services shuttling in and out of the United States—is good news for many sectors. Companies engaged in shipping, trucking, rail freight, delivery, and logistics(物流) have all been reporting better than expected results. The rising numbers signify growing vitality in foreign markets—when we import more stuff, it puts more cash in the hands of people around the world, and U.S. exports are rising because more foreigners have the ability to buy the things we produce and market. The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses, especially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\nAnd while exports always seem to lag, U.S. companies are becoming more involved in the global economy with each passing month. General Motors sells as many cars in China as in America each month. While that may not do much for imports, it does help GM's balance sheet—and hence makes the jobs of U.S.-based executives more stable.\nOne great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. That's to be expected, given what we've been through. But there's a bigger challenge. Can U.S.-based businesses, large and small, figure out how to get a piece of growing global demand? Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China, the best way to do that is through trade. It may seem obvious, but it's no longer enough simply to do business with our friends and neighbors here at home.\nCompanies and individuals who don't have a strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global trade, are shutting themselves out of the lion's share of economic opportunity in our world.", "51": "A recurring criticism of the UK's university sector is its perceived weakness in translating new knowledge into new products and services.\nRecently, the UK National Stem Cell Network warned the UK could lose its place among the world leaders in stem cell research unless adequate funding and legislation could be assured. We should take this concern seriously as universities are key in the national innovation system.\nHowever, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do enough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance of universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows that, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity.\nWhen viewed at the national level, the policy interventions of the past decade have helped transform the performance of U.K. universities. Evidence suggests the UK's position is much stronger than in the recent past and is still showing improvement. But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities. The evidence shows that a large number of universities have fallen off the back of the pack, a few perform strongly and the rest chase the leaders.\nThis type of uneven distribution is not peculiar to the U.K. and is mirrored across other economies. In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD graduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating long-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are research-led and commercially active. It seems clear that the concentration of research and commercialisation work creates differences between universities.\nThe core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of their research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits. In return for the scale of investment, they should share their expertise in order to build greater confidence in the sector.\nPart of the economic recovery of the UK will be driven by the next generation of research commercialisations spilling out of our universities. There are three dozen universities in the UK which are actively engaged in advanced research training and commercialisation work.\nIf there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities could, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle.", "52": "A recurring criticism of the UK's university sector is its perceived weakness in translating new knowledge into new products and services.\nRecently, the UK National Stem Cell Network warned the UK could lose its place among the world leaders in stem cell research unless adequate funding and legislation could be assured. We should take this concern seriously as universities are key in the national innovation system.\nHowever, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do enough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance of universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows that, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity.\nWhen viewed at the national level, the policy interventions of the past decade have helped transform the performance of U.K. universities. Evidence suggests the UK's position is much stronger than in the recent past and is still showing improvement. But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities. The evidence shows that a large number of universities have fallen off the back of the pack, a few perform strongly and the rest chase the leaders.\nThis type of uneven distribution is not peculiar to the U.K. and is mirrored across other economies. In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD graduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating long-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are research-led and commercially active. It seems clear that the concentration of research and commercialisation work creates differences between universities.\nThe core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of their research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits. In return for the scale of investment, they should share their expertise in order to build greater confidence in the sector.\nPart of the economic recovery of the UK will be driven by the next generation of research commercialisations spilling out of our universities. There are three dozen universities in the UK which are actively engaged in advanced research training and commercialisation work.\nIf there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities could, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle.", "53": "A recurring criticism of the UK's university sector is its perceived weakness in translating new knowledge into new products and services.\nRecently, the UK National Stem Cell Network warned the UK could lose its place among the world leaders in stem cell research unless adequate funding and legislation could be assured. We should take this concern seriously as universities are key in the national innovation system.\nHowever, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do enough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance of universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows that, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity.\nWhen viewed at the national level, the policy interventions of the past decade have helped transform the performance of U.K. universities. Evidence suggests the UK's position is much stronger than in the recent past and is still showing improvement. But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities. The evidence shows that a large number of universities have fallen off the back of the pack, a few perform strongly and the rest chase the leaders.\nThis type of uneven distribution is not peculiar to the U.K. and is mirrored across other economies. In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD graduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating long-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are research-led and commercially active. It seems clear that the concentration of research and commercialisation work creates differences between universities.\nThe core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of their research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits. In return for the scale of investment, they should share their expertise in order to build greater confidence in the sector.\nPart of the economic recovery of the UK will be driven by the next generation of research commercialisations spilling out of our universities. There are three dozen universities in the UK which are actively engaged in advanced research training and commercialisation work.\nIf there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities could, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle.", "54": "A recurring criticism of the UK's university sector is its perceived weakness in translating new knowledge into new products and services.\nRecently, the UK National Stem Cell Network warned the UK could lose its place among the world leaders in stem cell research unless adequate funding and legislation could be assured. We should take this concern seriously as universities are key in the national innovation system.\nHowever, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do enough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance of universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows that, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity.\nWhen viewed at the national level, the policy interventions of the past decade have helped transform the performance of U.K. universities. Evidence suggests the UK's position is much stronger than in the recent past and is still showing improvement. But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities. The evidence shows that a large number of universities have fallen off the back of the pack, a few perform strongly and the rest chase the leaders.\nThis type of uneven distribution is not peculiar to the U.K. and is mirrored across other economies. In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD graduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating long-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are research-led and commercially active. It seems clear that the concentration of research and commercialisation work creates differences between universities.\nThe core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of their research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits. In return for the scale of investment, they should share their expertise in order to build greater confidence in the sector.\nPart of the economic recovery of the UK will be driven by the next generation of research commercialisations spilling out of our universities. There are three dozen universities in the UK which are actively engaged in advanced research training and commercialisation work.\nIf there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities could, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle.", "55": "A recurring criticism of the UK's university sector is its perceived weakness in translating new knowledge into new products and services.\nRecently, the UK National Stem Cell Network warned the UK could lose its place among the world leaders in stem cell research unless adequate funding and legislation could be assured. We should take this concern seriously as universities are key in the national innovation system.\nHowever, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do enough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance of universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows that, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity.\nWhen viewed at the national level, the policy interventions of the past decade have helped transform the performance of U.K. universities. Evidence suggests the UK's position is much stronger than in the recent past and is still showing improvement. But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities. The evidence shows that a large number of universities have fallen off the back of the pack, a few perform strongly and the rest chase the leaders.\nThis type of uneven distribution is not peculiar to the U.K. and is mirrored across other economies. In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD graduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating long-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are research-led and commercially active. It seems clear that the concentration of research and commercialisation work creates differences between universities.\nThe core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of their research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits. In return for the scale of investment, they should share their expertise in order to build greater confidence in the sector.\nPart of the economic recovery of the UK will be driven by the next generation of research commercialisations spilling out of our universities. There are three dozen universities in the UK which are actively engaged in advanced research training and commercialisation work.\nIf there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities could, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-06-3-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the man say about the job in the States?", "options": ["A) It is well paid.", "B) It is stimulating.", "C) It is demanding.", "D) It is fairly secure."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is stimulating.\n【定位】Oh, yes. It'd be stimulating and it has some advantages.\n哦，是的。这份工作会很有干劲、令人兴奋，而且有一些好处。\n【信号】被问者对美国工作的直接应答。女方刚提到这份工作的待遇，男方紧接着应答 It'd be\nstimulating and it has some advantages，这是他对‘美国工作如何’的直接表态，正是答\n案落点；stimulating 一词即评价核心。\n【替换】stimulating ↔ stimulating\n评价词原义直接对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n对话开头男方说 I wouldn't get a very good salary——美国工作薪水并不好，It is well\npaid. 借了 salary 这个被反复念到的话题，却把意思说反了。\n借用原文：I wouldn't get a very good salary\nC) 未提及\nIt is demanding. 与正确项 It is stimulating. 词形相近、读音也容易在一遍过中混淆，但原\n文只说 stimulating，从没说这份工作 demanding(要求高、累人)，是凭相近词形设的陷\n阱。\nD) 张冠李戴\nsecure(稳定)是男方说葡萄牙工作时的话——It'd offer greater security，而 It is fairly\nsecure. 把这份稳定挪到了问的美国工作上；恰恰相反，美国工作是 on an annual\ncontract 年度合同，并不稳。\n借用原文：It'd offer greater security", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What benefit would the man get if he took the post in Sao Paulo?", "options": ["A) A quick promotion.", "B) Free accommodation.", "C) Moving expenses.", "D) A lighter workload."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A quick promotion.\n【定位】Yes. I'd get a promotion if I took a post there.\n是的。如果我接受那边的职位，我会得到晋升。\n【信号】被问者对圣保罗职位好处的直接应答。女方确认 Sao Paulo 在巴西后，男方紧接着回 I'd\nget a promotion if I took a post there，there 即指圣保罗，这句直接说出这份工作给的\n好处——promotion，是答案落点。\n【替换】a promotion ↔ A quick promotion.\npromotion 原词对应、选项加了修饰\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nFree accommodation. 直接盗用原文 free accommodation，但那是男方谈葡萄牙工作时\n说的薪资福利，不是圣保罗(巴西)的，听到原词就选会落入配对错位。\n借用原文：free accommodation from the firm\nC) 张冠李戴\nMoving expenses. 取自 moving expenses，但原文那句是女方推测葡萄牙工作会给搬家\n费，与圣保罗无关，是又一处把别份工作的福利移花接木。\n借用原文：you'd be given moving expenses for the Portugal job\nD) 未提及\nA lighter workload. 听上去像福利，但整段对话没有任何一处说哪份工作活儿更少，是凭\n空添的干扰，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man say is the disadvantage of working in Brazil?", "options": ["A) He has difficulty communicating with local people.", "B) He has to spend a lot more traveling back and forth.", "C) He has trouble adapting to the local weather.", "D) He has to sign a long-term contract."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has to sign a long-term contract.\n【定位】But there are some disadvantages. I'd have to do a five-year term there.\n但是也有一些缺点。我得在那里干满五年。\n【信号】转折 But 之后引出真正的缺点。男方先说升职是好处，随即用 But there are some\ndisadvantages 转折，转折后的 I'd have to do a five-year term there 才是答案句；But\n与 disadvantages 是双重信号。\n【替换】do a five-year term ↔ sign a long-term contract\n五年期=长期合约的概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHe has difficulty communicating with local people. 听着像海外工作常见难题，但对话\n从没提语言或沟通问题，是凭印象编的干扰。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文确谈了距离——I'd be a long way from home，He has to spend a lot more\ntraveling back and forth 蹭了这个距离话题，却偷换成‘往返花费更多’，原文说的是离家\n远、家里有事难回，并未说要花更多钱来回奔波。\n借用原文：I'd be a long way from home\nC) 未提及\nHe has trouble adapting to the local weather. 把缺点归到天气上，可整段对话没有一个\n字谈气候，是和原文完全无关的捏造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do we learn from the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) The woman will help the man make a choice.", "B) The man is going to attend a job interview.", "C) The man is in the process of job hunting.", "D) The woman sympathizes with the man."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题） The man is in the process of job hunting.\n【定位】Well, fortunately I don't have to make up my mind about any of them yet. In fact, I haven't\neven been called for an interview.\n嗯，幸好我现在还不必对这些职位中的任何一个做决定。事实上，我连面试通知都还没\n接到。\n【信号】对话收尾处男方的处境自陈。结尾男方说 I don't have to make up my mind about any\nof them yet 又补 I haven't even been called for an interview，这两句自陈点明他还远没\n到拍板阶段——正处在求职途中，是推断的依据。\n【替换】I haven't even been called for an interview ↔ in the process of job hunting\n连面试都没到=仍在求职途中\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nThe woman will help the man make a choice. 蹭了女方那句 you've got a difficult\nchoice to make，但女方只是评论‘你有个难选择’，并没说要帮他选，选择是男方自己的\n事。\n借用原文：I can see you've got a difficult choice to make\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe man is going to attend a job interview. 借了结尾 interview 这个词，但原文是 I\nhaven't even been called for an interview——连面试都还没被叫去，选项却说‘即将去面\n试’，恰好相反。\n借用原文：I haven't even been called for an interview\nD) 未提及\nThe woman sympathizes with the man. 说女方同情他，但女方结尾只说 I can see\nyou've got a difficult choice to make，更多是中性评论，加上她还说过 I'd love to go to\nBrazil 透着羡慕，谈不上同情。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is the man's purpose in making the phone call?", "options": ["A) To inquire about the interest rates at the woman's bank.", "B) To inquire about the current financial market situation.", "C) To see if he can find a job in the woman's company.", "D) To see if he can get a loan from the woman's bank."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） To inquire about the interest rates at the woman's bank.\n【定位】M: Well, I'm looking for the highest rate possible on a short-term certificate to\ndeposit account.\n男：嗯，我想找一个短期存单账户上尽可能高的利率。\n【信号】提问方诉求句 I'm looking for…。男士开口第一句 I'm looking for the highest rate\npossible 就是\"我想要……\"的诉求句，目的题的答案信号正是这种主动表达需求的句式，听\n到即定位。\n【替换】looking for the highest rate possible ↔ inquire about the interest rates\n\"想要高利率\"=\"咨询利率\"\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n录音男士要的是 the highest rate possible on a short-term certificate（某个存款的利\n率），不是 the current financial market situation（整体金融市场行情）；选项把\"问某\n存款利率\"偷换成\"问大市行情\"，范围放大了。\n借用原文：the highest rate possible on a short-term certificate to deposit account\nC) 未提及\nfind a job in the woman's company 在录音里毫无踪影，男士从头到尾谈的是存钱、利\n率，找工作纯属虚构，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nget a loan from the woman's bank 借用了银行名 Savings and Loan 里的 loan 一词，但\n男士是来\"存钱\"（deposit）的，不是来\"借钱\"（loan）的，听到 loan 就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：High Mountain Savings and Loan", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What can we know about rate and yield?", "options": ["A) There is no difference between rate and yield.", "B) The man has a good understanding of rate and yield.", "C) The rate is the percentage of simple interest paid on the money.", "D) The yield is only influenced by the amount of money."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The rate is the percentage of simple interest paid on the money.\n【定位】W: Well, sir. The rate is the percentage of simple interest paid on your money, and the yield is\nthe total percentage you will receive on your account if you leave your money with us, for at\nleast a year, at the same interest rate.\n女：嗯，先生。利率（rate）是按单利付给您本金的那个百分比；而收益率（yield）是您若\n把钱在我们这里存满至少一年、按同样利率算，您账户上能拿到的总百分比。\n【信号】下定义句 The rate is… and the yield is…。在男士问 I don’t understand the\ndifference 之后，女方用 The rate is the percentage of simple interest 这种\"A is… and\nB is…\"的下定义句作答，正是细节题该锁定的解释句。\n【替换】The rate is the percentage of simple interest paid on your money ↔\nThe rate is the percentage of simple interest paid on the money\n近乎原句，仅 your→the\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n男士专门问 I don’t understand the difference，女方随即分别定义二者，说明 rate 与\nyield 确有差别；There is no difference between rate and yield. 与录音意思正好相反。\n借用原文：I'm afraid I don't understand the difference.\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里是男士说 I don’t understand the difference（不懂区别），The man has a good\nunderstanding of rate and yield. 却说他\"很懂\"，把\"不懂\"说成\"懂\"，张冠李戴且反了向。\n借用原文：I don't understand the difference\nD)\n女方说 yield 是 if you leave your money with us, for at least a year，即收益率还跟\"存\n满一年\"这个时间条件有关；The yield is only influenced by the amount of money. 用\nonly 把影响因素缩到只剩金额，遗漏了时间，以偏概全。\n借用原文：if you leave your money with us, for at least a year", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the man most interested in?", "options": ["A) Long-term investment.", "B) A three-month deposit.", "C) Any high-interest deposit.", "D) Any high-yield investment."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A three-month deposit.\n【定位】M: Oh, well… Um… But I'm not interested in long-term investing and three months sounds\njust fine.\n男：哦，嗯……可我对长期投资不感兴趣，三个月就挺好。\n【信号】否定加偏好 not interested in… …sounds fine。男士先用否定 I’m not interested in\nlong-term investing 排掉长期，紧接 three months sounds just fine 正面表态三个\n月，\"先否后肯\"的落点在肯定那头，正是答案信号。\n【替换】three months sounds just fine ↔ A three-month deposit\n\"三个月就好\"=\"三个月存款\"\nnot interested in long-term investing ↔ A three-month deposit\n排掉长期反衬出短期\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nLong-term investment 直接搬了录音 long-term investing 的原词，可男士前面正是 not\ninterested in 它，听到 long-term 就选恰好踩中\"否定后还选\"的坑。\n借用原文：I'm not interested in long-term investing\nC)\nAny high-interest deposit 借了开头 the highest rate 的\"高利率\"概念，但他明确要的\n是\"短期 / 三个月\"，而非\"任何高息存款\"；any 一字把范围放到无限，与他具体的\"三个\n月\"诉求不符。\n借用原文：I'm looking for the highest rate possible\nD) 原词陷阱\nAny high-yield investment 拼了录音里出现过的 yield 和 investing 两个词，可男士要的\n是短期的三个月存款，且他刚否掉 long-term investing；any high-yield 既范围过大又方\n向不符。\n借用原文：not interested in long-term investing", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What did the woman do to persuade the man to deposit in her bank?", "options": ["A) She treated him to a meal.", "B) She gave him loans at low rates.", "C) She offered him dining coupons.", "D) She raised interest rates for him."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She offered him dining coupons.\n【定位】W: Or, could we persuade you to save with us if we offered you a dining coupon book that\nwill get you a 25% discount at many of the area's leading restaurants and cost you only 12\ndollars and 95 cents?\n女：或者，如果我们送您一本餐饮优惠券册，能让您在本地区许多一流餐厅打七五折、且只\n花您 12.95 美元，能不能说动您在我们这儿存钱呢？\n【信号】劝说动词 persuade + offered 的具体物。题干的 persuade 在录音 could we persuade\nyou to save 里原词出现，紧跟的 offered you a dining coupon book 就是她做的那个具\n体动作，定位即此句。\n【替换】offered you a dining coupon book ↔ She offered him dining coupons\n\"给餐券册\"=\"给餐券\"\n【排除】A)\nShe treated him to a meal. 听感和\"餐\"有关，但录音是 offered you a dining coupon\nbook（给打折餐券），不是她亲自请吃饭；treat to a meal（请客）和给券是两码事。\n借用原文：a dining coupon book\nB) 原词陷阱\nShe gave him loans at low rates. 拼了银行名里的 loan 和全篇的 rate，可女方给的是餐\n券，全程没提给他低息贷款；这是把听过的金融词拼成一个没发生的动作。\n借用原文：High Mountain Savings and Loan\nD) 原词陷阱\nShe raised interest rates for him. 借了通篇的 interest / rate，但女方报的利率是固定档\n位、并未\"为他提高\"，她最后的挽留手段是给餐券而非加息。\n借用原文：a rate of 6,496 on a thirteen-week account", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say about the international coffee institute?", "options": ["A) It is a Portuguese company selling coffee in New York.", "B) Its most important task is to conduct coffee studies.", "C) It represents several countries that export coffee.", "D) Its role is to regulate international coffee prices."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It represents several countries that export coffee.\n【定位】He works for an international coffee institute. It represents Brazil and several other countries\nthat export coffee.\n他在一家国际咖啡机构工作。该机构代表巴西以及其他几个出口咖啡的国家。\n【信号】对机构的直接定义句。录音用 It represents Brazil and several other countries that\nexport coffee. 直接给机构下定义，这种 \"It + 表职能的动词\" 的定性句就是答案信号，听\n到对题中对象的定义句即可锁定。\n【替换】It represents Brazil and several other countries that export coffee. ↔\nIt represents several countries that export coffee.\n近乎原句的同义压缩\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到主人公 lives and works in New York 且其业务与咖啡有关，A 便把 \"在纽\n约\"\"咖啡\" 拼成 a Portuguese company selling coffee in New York，但机构是国际咖啡\n机构、不是 \"葡萄牙公司卖咖啡\"，葡萄牙只是后文人物的母语来源，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Vaukin de Silva is a Brazilian who lives and works in New York.\nB) 未提及\n录音说机构想让更多人喝咖啡、后来搞广告宣传，B 却说其首要任务是 conduct coffee\nstudies（做咖啡研究），录音通篇没提研究，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音后文大量谈到价格上涨 The shortage has caused prices to go very high.，D 便把\n\"价格\" 接到机构身上说它 regulate international coffee prices（调控价格），但录音只说\n机构代表出口国、想推动消费，从没说它调控价格，盗用 prices 一词答非所问。\n借用原文：The shortage has caused prices to go very high.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What caused the recent shortage of coffee throughout the world?", "options": ["A) The freezing weather in Brazil.", "B) The impact of global warming.", "C) The increased coffee consumption.", "D) The fluctuation of coffee prices."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The freezing weather in Brazil.\n【定位】A period of freezing weather destroyed a large part of Brazil's coffee crop. As a result, there\nhas been a shortage of coffee throughout the world.\n一段时间的严寒天气毁掉了巴西大部分咖啡作物。结果，全世界都出现了咖啡短缺。\n【信号】as a result 锁定因果。录音用 As a result, there has been a shortage of coffee\nthroughout the world. 中的 as a result 把前句 freezing weather destroyed a large\npart of Brazil's coffee crop 标成原因、后句标成结果，这个因果信号词就是答案定位点。\n【替换】A period of freezing weather destroyed a large part of Brazil's coffee crop. ↔\nThe freezing weather in Brazil.\n把完整因果句概括成名词短语\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说的是 freezing weather（严寒），B 却说 The impact of global warming.（全球变\n暖），方向正好相反；考生若只听到 \"天气\" 没听清是冷是暖，就可能被它带跑，与原文相\n反。\n借用原文：A period of freezing weather destroyed a large part of Brazil's coffee crop.\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实希望 more people to drink more coffee，C 便顺势说短缺是因 The increased\ncoffee consumption.（消费增加），但录音明说短缺源于天气毁了收成，消费多是机构的\n愿望、不是短缺的原因，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The institute wants more people to drink more coffee.\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里价格高是短缺造成的结果 The shortage has caused prices to go very high.，D 却\n说 The fluctuation of coffee prices.（价格波动）是短缺的原因，因果颠倒。\n借用原文：The shortage has caused prices to go very high.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do we learn about Vaukin de Silva?", "options": ["A) He is doing a bachelor's degree.", "B) He is young, handsome and single.", "C) He is a heavy coffee drinker.", "D) He is tall, rich and intelligent."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He is young, handsome and single.\n【定位】He's young, only 27 years old, and he's a good-looking bachelor.\n他很年轻，才 27 岁，是个相貌出众的单身汉。\n【信号】集中描人的并列特征句。录音用 He's young, only 27 years old, and he's a good-\nlooking bachelor. 一口气并列给出年龄、外貌、婚姻三项特征，这种集中描人的句子就是\n人物细节题的答案句。\n【替换】young ↔ young\n原词对应\ngood-looking ↔ handsome\n同义词替换\nbachelor ↔ single\n名词转形容词的同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音只说他 27 岁、年轻，A 却推断他 doing a bachelor's degree（在读学士学位），录\n音中的 bachelor 是 \"单身汉\"、不是 \"学士\"，A 把这个一词多义的词理解错了，过度推断。\n借用原文：he's a good-looking bachelor\nC) 原词陷阱\n全篇都在讲咖啡，C 便说他是 a heavy coffee drinker（重度咖啡爱好者），但录音只说咖\n啡是他的业务、从没说他本人嗜咖啡，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：coffee is Vaukin's business\nD) 未提及\nD 用 tall, rich and intelligent 三个形容词模仿 B 的三连结构，但录音说的是 young、\ngood-looking、bachelor，tall、rich、intelligent 三词录音一个都没提，靠句式眼熟蒙\n人，未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What has occupied Vaukin's mind for the last few weeks?", "options": ["A) A visit to several coffee-growing plantations.", "B) Coffee prices and his advertising campaign.", "C) A vacation on some beautiful tropical beach.", "D) A quick promotion and a handsome income."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Coffee prices and his advertising campaign.\n【定位】The last few weeks, however, he's been too busy to think about anything except coffee prices\nand his advertising campaign.\n然而最近这几周，他忙得除了咖啡价格和他的广告宣传活动之外，别的什么都顾不上想。\n【信号】时间状语加 however 转折锁定。录音以 The last few weeks, however, he's been too\nbusy to think about anything except coffee prices and his advertising campaign. 用\n时间状语 The last few weeks 加转折词 however，把话题从前面怀念海滩的旧情绪拉回\n\"现在只顾两件事\"，这句才是答案落点。\n【替换】coffee prices and his advertising campaign ↔ Coffee prices and his advertising campaign.\n近乎原句照搬\nhe's been too busy to think about anything except ↔ B\n排他句锁定唯一在意之事\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 A visit to several coffee-growing plantations（参观咖啡种植园），借了全篇的\ncoffee 话题造势，但录音从没提他去参观种植园，无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确说他有时 misses the beaches and tropical weather in Rio de Janeiro，C 便据此\n选 A vacation on some beautiful tropical beach，但那是 sometimes 的怀念、且被\nhowever 转折掉了，不是最近占据心思的事，张冠李戴又错配时间。\n借用原文：Vaukin misses the beaches and tropical weather in Rio de Janeiro\nD) 过度推断\n录音说他被派去负责广告宣传 Vaukin was put in charge of an advertising campaign，D\n顺势推断他得到 A quick promotion and a handsome income（快速升职和丰厚收\n入），但录音从没提升职或收入，过度推断。\n借用原文：Vaukin was put in charge of an advertising campaign.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why did the couple fail to get to the cruise in time?", "options": ["A) They were held up in a traffic jam.", "B) They boarded a wrong coach in a hurry.", "C) They were late for the first morning bus.", "D) They were delayed by the train for hours."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They were delayed by the train for hours.\n【定位】Because the train was scheduled to arrive in Miami, Florida several hours before the sailing,\nwe didn't think we'd have any reason to worry about getting to the dock on time. We\nshould've known that long distance trains are often many hours late.\n因为火车原定在开船前几个小时就到达佛罗里达州的迈阿密，我们觉得没理由担心赶不上到\n码头的时间。我们本该想到，长途火车常常会晚点好几个小时。\n【信号】转折式悔悟句 We should've…才是真因。前半句 we didn't think we'd have any reason\nto worry 是当时的乐观预期，真正的原因藏在后面悔悟的 We should've known that\nlong distance trains are often many hours late——火车晚点好几个小时才是没赶上船的\n根子。\n【替换】long distance trains are often many hours late ↔ delayed by the train for hours\n“火车晚点好几个小时”同义改写成“被火车耽误数小时”\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey were held up in a traffic jam. 说的是堵车，但录音从头到尾只讲火车晚点，没有任\n何提到开车遇堵的内容，属凭常识臆造的原因。\nB) 未提及\nThey boarded a wrong coach in a hurry. 说匆忙上错了车，录音只说火车晚点害他们误了\n船，从没说他们上错车，是无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey were late for the first morning bus. 借了录音“该早点出发”的影子（We should've\ntaken an earlier one 指早一班火车），却偷换成赶不上头班巴士，录音里压根没有 bus，\n是把交通工具张冠李戴的迷惑项。\n借用原文：We should've taken an earlier one to allow extra time", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the speaker say about their trip this year?", "options": ["A) It was canceled because of an unexpected strike.", "B) It was spoiled by poor accommodations.", "C) It was the most exciting trip they ever had.", "D) It was postponed due to terrible weather."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was canceled because of an unexpected strike.\n【定位】We should be joining that relaxing well-planned trip right now. How could we have known\nthere would be a strike against our airline?\n我们现在本该正在享受那趟轻松又安排周到的旅行才对。我们怎么会料到航空公司会发生罢\n工呢？\n【信号】反问句 How could we have known…道出意外是罢工。We should be joining that\nrelaxing well-planned trip right now 用“本该正在……（却没有）”暗示行程泡汤，紧跟的\n反问 How could we have known there would be a strike against our airline 点出搅黄\n行程的意外正是航空公司罢工。\n【替换】there would be a strike against our airline ↔ canceled because of an unexpected strike\n“航空公司罢工”＝“因意外罢工被取消”\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nIt was spoiled by poor accommodations. 说被糟糕住宿毁了，录音里和住宿沾边的是去\n年误船后 we had to pay for a hotel room（被迫掏钱住酒店），那是去年的事、也并非\n“住宿差”，本题问的是今年，时间和事由都对不上。\n借用原文：we had to pay for a hotel room and meals in the city\nC) 与原文相反\nIt was the most exciting trip they ever had. 说是最精彩的一次旅行，但今年行程因罢工\n压根没成行（We should be joining that relaxing well-planned trip right now 是“本该\n却没”的遗憾语气），整段是抱怨，不可能是“最棒”，与原文相反。\n借用原文：We should be joining that relaxing well-planned trip right now\nD) 偷换概念\nIt was postponed due to terrible weather. 也讲行程被某原因耽搁，方向蹭对了，但把原\n因偷换成 terrible weather（坏天气）；录音明说今年的祸首是罢工，从未提天气，原因张\n冠李戴。\n借用原文：there would be a strike against our airline", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What will the speaker do in the future for a vacation?", "options": ["A) Go overseas.", "B) Stay at home.", "C) Take romantic cruises.", "D) Take escorted trips."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Stay at home.\n【定位】But to tell you the truth, we don't want to go anywhere. We're planning to spend our vacations\nat home for a long time to come.\n但说实话，我们哪儿也不想去了。我们打算今后很长一段时间都在家里过假期。\n【信号】转折 But＋未来时间状语，落在“在家过假期”。前一句 we ought to get more travel\nexperience 是个虚晃的应该，紧跟 But to tell you the truth, we don't want to go\nanywhere 用 But 转折道出真实想法，再以 spend our vacations at home for a long\ntime to come（今后很长一段时间在家过假期）落定将来的打算。\n【替换】spend our vacations at home for a long time to come ↔ Stay at home\n“在家过假期”概括为“待在家”\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nGo overseas. 是出国玩，但录音结尾明说 we don't want to go anywhere（哪儿也不想去\n了），方向正好相反。\n借用原文：we don't want to go anywhere\nC) 原词陷阱\nTake romantic cruises. 直接搬了录音第一段 a romantic cruise 这个抢眼原词，但那是去\n年想去（且没去成）的旅行，不是今后的打算；本题问的是 future，录音结尾说要在家，并\n不会再去坐邮轮。\n借用原文：a romantic cruise to several Caribbean islands\nD) 原词陷阱\nTake escorted trips. 搬了录音今年那次 an escorted tour 的原词，但那是今年已经尝试\n（且因罢工泡汤）的方式，不是今后的打算；录音结尾明说今后要待在家，不再去跟团。\n借用原文：an escorted tour", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What makes dolphins different from other sea animals?", "options": ["A) Their extraordinary intelligence.", "B) The special bones in their fins.", "C) The way they communicate.", "D) The way they get water."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The way they communicate.\n【定位】One unique characteristic that makes them different from other sea animals is the way that\nthey communicate.\n让它们区别于其他海洋动物的一个独有特征，就是它们沟通交流的方式。\n【信号】unique characteristic…different 是与众不同的强调信号。One unique characteristic\nthat makes them different from other sea animals 直接照搬了题干里 different from\nother sea animals 的措辞，又用 unique characteristic 强调独有，这种点名与众不同的句\n子就是答案句信号，紧跟其后的 the way that they communicate 即答案落点。\n【替换】the way that they communicate ↔ The way they communicate.\n近乎原句、仅省去关系词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头确说过 one of the most intelligent animals on the planet is the dolphin，\nTheir extraordinary intelligence. 借了 intelligent 这个原词诱你选，但智力是引入语、并\n非本题问的让它与其他海洋动物区别的那项特征，属答非所问。\n借用原文：one of the most intelligent animals on the planet is the dolphin\nB) 张冠李戴\nThe special bones in their fins. 取自后文 the bones in their fins are similar to those in\nhuman arms and hands，鳍骨确是录音讲的一项特征，但录音明确用 unique\ncharacteristic…different 点中的是沟通方式，鳍骨那段并未被冠以与其他海洋动物相区别\n的标签，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the bones in their fins are similar to those in human arms and hands\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe way they get water. 对应 they do not drink the salt water around them. Their\nbodies absorb water from the wide variety of creatures they eat，取水方式录音确有，\n但同样不是那句 unique…different 点中的项，被挪来当本题答案属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：they do not drink the salt water around them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why do many people enjoy collecting dolphin souvenirs?", "options": ["A) They love to keep them as pets just like cats and dogs.", "B) They love them but cannot live with them.", "C) They can find many stores selling such souvenirs.", "D) They believe such souvenirs can bring good luck."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They love them but cannot live with them.\n【定位】Loving a dolphin is an easy thing to do but, unlike a dog or a cat, the dolphin cannot come\nhome with you from the ocean. That is why many people like collecting dolphin souvenirs.\n爱上海豚很容易，但与狗或猫不同，海豚没法像它们那样跟你从海里回家。正因如此，许多\n人才喜欢收藏海豚纪念品。\n【信号】but 转折加 That is why 双重因果信号。but, unlike a dog or a cat, the dolphin cannot\ncome home with you 先用 but 转折点出爱却不能共同生活的矛盾，再用 That is why\nmany people like collecting dolphin souvenirs 的 That is why 把前因直接接到收藏纪念\n品上，转折加显性因果两个信号叠在一起，锁定前半句即原因。\n【替换】Loving a dolphin is an easy thing to do ↔ They love them\n动名词转人称、同义\nthe dolphin cannot come home with you from the ocean ↔ cannot live with them\n不能带回家=不能共同生活，概括改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey love to keep them as pets just like cats and dogs. 借了录音里 unlike a dog or a\ncat 的猫狗原词诱选，但录音原意是与猫狗不同、海豚不能带回家养，选项却说像猫狗一样\n当宠物养，恰好说反。\n借用原文：unlike a dog or a cat, the dolphin cannot come home with you from the ocean\nC) 答非所问\nThey can find many stores selling such souvenirs. 借了 One can find many such\nthings in almost every shop around the world 的原词，但那句讲的是纪念品到处买得\n到，是收藏带来的现象、不是人们想收藏的原因，答非所问。\n借用原文：One can find many such things in almost every shop around the world\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey believe such souvenirs can bring good luck. 里的 good luck 取自前文古希腊水手\n见海豚 they believed they would have good luck，但那讲的是航海好运，录音从没说收\n藏纪念品能带来好运，本题范围内属未提及。\n借用原文：they believed they would have good luck", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What did ancient people do with dolphins?", "options": ["A) They made them swim around their ships.", "B) They painted their images on some items.", "C) They hunted them in great numbers.", "D) They trained them to perform tricks."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They painted their images on some items.\n【定位】Even the ancient people painted the images of dolphins on their vases.\n甚至连古人都在自己的花瓶上画上了海豚的图像。\n【信号】主语 ancient people 与题干主体直接对应。Even the ancient people painted the\nimages of dolphins on their vases 这句主语就是 the ancient people，与题干 ancient\npeople 直接对上，Even 又起强调，定位极清晰，其谓语 painted the images 就是答案\n动作。\n【替换】painted the images of dolphins on their vases ↔ painted their images on some items\nvases 概括为 items、其余近原词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey made them swim around their ships. 借了开头 if sailors saw dolphins swim by\ntheir ships 里 swim 与 ships 的原词，但录音是水手看见海豚自己游过船边，并非古人让海\n豚绕船游，既是动作主体错位也属过度引申。\n借用原文：if sailors saw dolphins swim by their ships when they were starting out on a voyage\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey hunted them in great numbers. 借了 dolphins are sometimes hunted in places\nlike Japan 的 hunted，但捕猎讲的是当今日本之事、主语不是 ancient people，把今人之\n事安到古人头上属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：dolphins are sometimes hunted in places like Japan\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey trained them to perform tricks. 借了 trained to perform tricks 的原词，但那讲的\n是如今把海豚圈养起来训练表演，与古人无关，仍是把当代行为错配给古人的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：trained to perform tricks", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What's happening to the dolphins?", "options": ["A) They are hunted in many places except Japan.", "B) They have been protected thanks to literature and film.", "C) They have adapted to the life in captivity.", "D) They are losing their habitat and clean sea."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are losing their habitat and clean sea.\n【定位】However, dolphins are sometimes hunted in places like Japan, in an activity known as\ndolphin drive hunting. Besides drive hunting, they also face threats from habitat loss and\nmarine pollution.\n然而，海豚有时会在日本等地遭到捕猎，这种行为被称为驱赶式捕猎。除了驱赶式捕猎，它\n们还面临着栖息地丧失和海洋污染的威胁。\n【信号】However 转折加 face threats 收尾的现状信号。末段开头 However, dolphins are\nsometimes hunted in places like Japan 用 However 把语气从前文的受喜爱转到当下困\n境，再以 they also face threats from habitat loss and marine pollution 列出现实威\n胁，转折加 face threats 正是现状题答案句的信号。\n【替换】face threats from habitat loss ↔ losing their habitat\nhabitat loss 名词化还原为动词、同义\nmarine pollution ↔ clean sea\n海洋污染=失去洁净海域，正反对调\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are hunted in many places except Japan. 借了 hunted in places like Japan 的\nhunted 与 Japan，但录音是在日本等地被捕猎，选项却说除日本外的许多地方，把 like\nJapan（包括日本）改成 except Japan（排除日本），范围正好相反。\n借用原文：dolphins are sometimes hunted in places like Japan\nB) 过度推断\nThey have been protected thanks to literature and film. 借了 Dolphins occasionally\nfeature in literature and film 的原词，但录音只说海豚偶尔出现在文学影视中，从未说因\n此受到保护，protected 与 thanks to 的因果是凭空加上去的。\n借用原文：Dolphins occasionally feature in literature and film\nC) 与原文相反\nThey have adapted to the life in captivity. 借了 kept in captivity 的原词，但录音说圈养\n下繁殖不佳、常在被捕数月内死亡，恰恰是没能适应，选项说已适应与原文相反。\n借用原文：the animals often die within a few months of capture", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What's the possible advantage of virus therapy according to Prof. Seymour?", "options": ["A) It won't have any side-effect.", "B) It can kill drug-resistant tumours.", "C) It can be as effective as conventional treatments.", "D) It can stop cancer cells from spreading in the body."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can kill drug-resistant tumours.\n【定位】Preliminary research on mice shows that the viruses work well on tumours resistant to\nstandard cancer drugs. \"It's an interesting possibility that they may have an advantage in\nkilling drug-resistant tumours, which could be quite different to anything we've had before.\"\n在小鼠身上的初步研究表明，这些病毒对那些抵抗标准抗癌药物的肿瘤很有效。「一个有趣\n的可能是，它们在杀死耐药肿瘤方面或许具有优势，这可能和我们以往拥有的任何手段都大\n不相同。」\n【信号】原文直接出现 advantage 这个题眼词。原文 they may have an advantage in killing\ndrug-resistant tumours 里的 advantage 与题干 advantage 是同一个词，教授亲口说出\n的这句优势论断就是答案句，听到 advantage 就该锁定此处。\n【替换】an advantage in killing drug-resistant tumours ↔ It can kill drug-resistant tumours\n名词短语 killing 改写成动词 kill\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文首段确实念到 while avoiding some of the side-effects（避免一些副作用），听到\nside-effect 就想选 A；但原文只说避免一些副作用，绝不等于 It won't have any side-\neffect. 的毫无任何副作用，程度被夸大，且这不是教授讲的那个 advantage。\n借用原文：while avoiding some of the side-effects\nC) 偷换概念\n原文教授说 something which could be many times more effective than regular\nchemotherapy（比常规化疗有效许多倍），选项 It can be as effective as conventional\ntreatments. 却说成和常规疗法一样有效；more effective（更有效）被偷改成 as effective\nas（同等有效），程度被压低，与原文相反。\n借用原文：something which could be many times more effective than regular chemotherapy\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文多处念到 spread（has spread throughout the body、spread through the\nblood），听到 spread 容易选 It can stop cancer cells from spreading in the body.；但\n原文的 spread 说的是病毒在血液里扩散、或癌症已扩散，从没说这疗法能阻止癌细胞扩\n散，属盗词又答非所问。\n借用原文：spread through the blood and reach tumours wherever they are", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What's the big challenge facing virus therapy?", "options": ["A) How to find a way to deliver viruses to tumours effectively.", "B) How to inject viruses directly into tumours.", "C) How to allow viruses to do what chemotherapy drugs do.", "D) How to strengthen the body's immune system."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） How to find a way to deliver viruses to tumours effectively.\n【定位】The big hurdle has always been to find a way to deliver viruses to tumours via the\nbloodstream without the body's immune system destroying them on the way.\n一直以来最大的障碍是：找到一种办法，让病毒经由血液被送到肿瘤处，同时又不被身体的\n免疫系统在半路上摧毁。\n【信号】big hurdle 这个表障碍的强调信号。原文 The big hurdle has always been 里的 big\nhurdle（最大障碍）正是 challenge 的同义信号词，has always been 又加重了它一贯是难\n题的语气，听到这种讲难处的措辞就锁定其后内容为答案句。\n【替换】to find a way to deliver viruses to tumours via the bloodstream without the body's immune system\ndestroying them\n↔ How to find a way to deliver viruses to tumours effectively\n把不被免疫系统摧毁概括成 effectively\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文第四段念到 American scientists have previously injected viruses directly into\ntumours，听到 inject directly 就想选 How to inject viruses directly into tumours.；但\n原文说直接注射是已有做法、且在癌症扩散时行不通，它不是要解决的难题，而是被替代的\n旧法，盗词又答非所问。\n借用原文：American scientists have previously injected viruses directly into tumours\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文第五段念到 allowing the viruses to do what chemotherapy drugs do，听到 do\nwhat chemotherapy drugs do 就想选 How to allow viruses to do what chemotherapy\ndrugs do.；但这是教授的解决办法（让病毒像化疗药一样随血流到处走），是难题被攻克\n后的结果，不是 challenge 本身。\n借用原文：effectively allowing the viruses to do what chemotherapy drugs do\nD) 与原文相反\n原文反复念到 immune system（without the body's immune system destroying\nthem），听到 immune system 就想选 How to strengthen the body's immune\nsystem.；但原文说的是要躲开/瞒过免疫系统、别让它毁掉病毒，绝不是增强免疫系统，方\n向恰好相反。\n借用原文：to mask the virus from the body's immune system", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does Prof. Seymour want to do with virus therapy?", "options": ["A) To use it to cure 75% of the patients with malignant cancers.", "B) To make it accepted by the patients with incurable cancers.", "C) To apply it to those with secondary cancers.", "D) To apply it to treat all kinds of cancers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To apply it to treat all kinds of cancers.\n【定位】Though the approach will be examined at first for cancers that do not respond to conventional\ntreatments, Prof. Seymour hopes that one day it will be applied to all cancers.\n尽管这种方法起初只会用于那些对常规疗法无反应的癌症，但西摩教授希望有朝一日它能被\n用于所有癌症。\n【信号】hopes that 这个表愿望的信号 + Though 让步转折。原文 Prof. Seymour hopes that\none day it will be applied to all cancers 里的 hopes 直接对应题干 want to do；前半\nThough the approach will be examined at first for cancers that do not respond to\nconventional treatments 是让步，真正的打算落在 Though 之后的 hopes 这句，转折后\n才是教授真意。\n【替换】it will be applied to all cancers ↔ To apply it to treat all kinds of cancers\napplied to all cancers 与 apply to all kinds 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文第六段念到 75% of them go on to die from secondary cancers 和 malignant\ncancers，听到 75%、malignant 就想选 To use it to cure 75% of the patients with\nmalignant cancers.；但原文这个 75% 说的是恶性癌病人有 75% 会死于继发癌，是个统计\n惨况，根本不是教授要治愈 75% 病人的打算，盗数字又答非所问。\n借用原文：75% of them go on to die from secondary cancers\nB) 未提及\n选项 To make it accepted by the patients with incurable cancers. 说要让无法治愈的癌\n症病人接受它，原文虽提到 cancers that do not respond to conventional treatments\n（对常规疗法无反应的癌），但从没说教授的目标是让病人接受它（accepted by the\npatients），accepted 这层意思原文未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文第六段念到 especially useful for secondary cancers，听到 secondary cancers 就想\n选 To apply it to those with secondary cancers.；但原文只说对继发癌特别有用，教授的\n最终打算却是用于 all cancers（所有癌），C 把范围偷缩成只用于继发癌，与教授真意不\n符。\n借用原文：The therapy would be especially useful for secondary cancers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What can be learnt about Amazon.com's Kindle library-lending service?", "options": ["A) It allows users of some Kindle devices to borrow books from local libraries.", "B) It has benefited more than eleven thousand local libraries in the US.", "C) It has led to a debate between publishers and libraries over e-book lending.", "D) It aims at allowing everyone to read books anytime they like."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It allows users of some Kindle devices to borrow books from local libraries.\n【定位】Millions of users of the Kindle reader and app can now borrow Kindle books from their local\npublic library.\n数百万 Kindle 阅读器和应用的用户，现在可以从他们当地的公共图书馆借阅 Kindle 电子\n书了。\n【信号】开篇介绍对象的功能定义句。篇章独白开头紧接服务名 launched its Kindle library-\nlending service 之后，就用一句话说清这服务让用户做什么——can now borrow Kindle\nbooks from their local public library，这种对象功能定义句正是细节题答案最常落脚处。\n【替换】can now borrow Kindle books from their local public library ↔\nborrow books from local libraries\n近乎同义、仅作概括化改写\nMillions of users of the Kindle reader and app ↔ users of some Kindle devices\n下位概括为上位\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说服务将通过 more than eleven thousand local libraries 的网站提供，eleven\nthousand 修饰的是覆盖的图书馆数量；选项 It has benefited more than eleven\nthousand local libraries 把这同一个数字偷换成已经使超过一万一千家图书馆受益，\nbenefited 的结果是录音没说的。\n借用原文：more than eleven thousand local libraries across the country\nC) 答非所问\n录音确实说亚马逊入局 likely to reopen a debate between publishers and libraries，但\n这是该服务可能引发的后续争论，而非问的这项服务本身是什么；It has led to a debate…\n把衍生后果错当对服务本身的描述，且 likely to reopen 只是可能、并非已经导致。\n借用原文：Amazon's entry is likely to reopen a debate between publishers and libraries over e-\nbook lending\nD) 过度推断\nIt aims at allowing everyone to read books anytime they like. 听上去像图书馆的理想，\n但 everyone… anytime 这种无限制说法是录音里图书馆方的愿望（make books available\nto everyone, all the time），并非这项服务的宗旨，把别人的愿景安到服务目标上属拔高\n与张冠李戴。\n借用原文：make books available to everyone, all the time, with no limitations", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What can libraries do according to the \"first sale\" law?", "options": ["A) They can purchase any kind of media product.", "B) They can lend books to readers repeatedly.", "C) They can deal with digital products as they like.", "D) They can limit the time the readers keep a certain book."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They can lend books to readers repeatedly.\n【定位】This law is what permits libraries to lend books over and over again without having to pay\npublishers each time.\n正是这条法律允许图书馆一次又一次地出借书籍，而无需每次都向出版商付费。\n【信号】permits 直接点明规则赋予的权利。答案句用 permits libraries to lend books over and\nover again，permits（允许）是规则赋权的明确信号词，题干问 what can libraries do，\n落点就在 permits 后面的那个动作上。\n【替换】lend books over and over again ↔ lend books to readers repeatedly\nover and over again = repeatedly 同义替换\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音里 buy any kind of media product 讲的是任何买了媒介产品的人（you）在 first\nsale 法下能随意处置它，主体是买家个人；选项 They can purchase any kind of media\nproduct. 把这话安到图书馆头上、还把重点从买后处置偷换成能购买任何产品，主体与重点\n双错。\n借用原文：Once you buy any kind of media product such as a book or a CD or a DVD\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 They can deal with digital products as they like. 借用了录音里的 digital\nproducts；但录音恰恰强调 first sale 法 does not include digital products——对数字产\n品并不适用，所以图书馆并不能随意处置数字产品，选项把否定说成了肯定。\n借用原文：it does not include digital products\nD) 答非所问\nThey can limit the time the readers keep a certain book. 描述的限制读者持有时长，对\n应的是录音里 digital rights management 技术能在到期后让电子书不可读，那是数字版权\n管理的效果、且是限制图书馆的做法，与 first sale 法允许图书馆做什么无关，答非所问。\n借用原文：Technology known as digital rights management can make e-books unreadable once\nthey have reached a certain time or user limit", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How did HarperCollins protect the e-book industry and its own book sales?", "options": ["A) By allowing libraries to purchase e-books for lending in perpetuity.", "B) By adopting the technology known as digital rights management.", "C) By requiring libraries to purchase more of its e-book.", "D) By persuading the critics to support the publishers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By requiring libraries to purchase more of its e-book.\n【定位】HarperCollins announced that they were only going to allow e-books to be lent out twenty-six\ntimes, and then they would have to be purchased by the libraries again.\nHarperCollins 宣布，他们只允许电子书被外借二十六次，之后图书馆就必须重新购买这些\n电子书。\n【信号】主体名 + only…then 限定句即做法落点。答案句紧跟主体名 HarperCollins，用 only…\ntwenty-six times… then…purchased… again 这种限定加后续要求的句式说清其具体做\n法，正是问 How 时的落点；后一句 Apart from HarperCollins 把它与其他出版商区隔\n开，更确认这做法专属 HarperCollins。\n【替换】then they would have to be purchased by the libraries again ↔\nrequiring libraries to purchase more of its e-book\n被动转主动 + again 概括为 more\nonly going to allow e-books to be lent out twenty-six times ↔\nrequiring libraries to purchase more\n借满次数即触发重购，因果对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nBy allowing libraries to purchase e-books for lending in perpetuity. 几乎照抄录音\nallowing libraries to purchase e-books for lending in perpetuity，但录音明说这是\nApart from HarperCollins 其他出版商的做法，恰恰与 HarperCollins 相反；把别家的永\n久出借做法安到 HarperCollins 头上，正是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Apart from HarperCollins, publishers are allowing libraries to purchase e-books for\nlending in perpetuity\nB) 原词陷阱\nBy adopting the technology known as digital rights management. 搬来录音里的专有\n术语；但录音 Technology known as digital rights management 只是背景介绍这类技术\n能限制电子书，并未说 HarperCollins 采用它来保护销售，把背景技术冒充成本题主体的具\n体手段。\n借用原文：Technology known as digital rights management can make e-books unreadable\nD) 与原文相反\nBy persuading the critics to support the publishers. 与录音相反：录音说 critics did\nnot see it that way，批评者并不认同，HarperCollins 并未去说服批评者支持，更没把它\n作为保护手段，属与原文相反加未提及。\n借用原文：Bill Rosenblatt says critics did not see it that way", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) controlled\n动词\n【词性槽】no longer + 过去分词，与 hidden 并列\n【依据】信息被旅行社『掌控』正是 90 年代行业旧态\nWith information no longer controlled by travel agents or hidden in business\nnetworks, the travel industry was revolutionized：在线旅游网站出现前，价格信息长期\n由旅行社『把持』，与后文 transparency（透明化）形成对比，controlled 准确无误。\n【竞争词】H) overstated overstated = 夸大；与 by travel agents 的『把持』语义对不上\n信息被『夸大』并不能解释为何旅游业被透明化革命；overstated 留给第 34 题 cannot be __\n的固定搭配 cannot be overstated（影响不可低估）。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) slash\n动词\n【词性槽】helped + 动词原形（to 省略）\n【依据】slash prices = 大幅压价，正是『透明化导致价格下降』的结果\ngreater transparency helped slash prices：信息透明 → 消费者能横向比价 → 商家被迫\n降价；slash 比 cut/reduce 更强烈，呼应『被革命』的力度。\n【竞争词】D) entail entail = 必然涉及/需要，无法搭配 prices 表降价\nentail prices 在英语里不成立；entail 通常接 risks / costs / responsibilities 这类抽象后果，且\n方向是『带来某事』而非『把价格砍下去』。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— entail 词性对，但搭配错。entail 与 slash 均为动词原形，\n词性槽匹配；但 helped __(27)__ prices 要求与 prices 成搭配的强动词，slash prices 是地\n道商业短语，entail prices 不成立。防坑要诀——动 + 具体宾语必查固定搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) specializing\n动词\n【词性槽】破折号插入语，做 Online rating platforms 的后置定语\n【依据】specialize in = 专门做某领域，与四个具体行业列举完美咬合\nOnline rating platforms—__(28)__ in hotels, restaurants, apartments, and taxis：平台\n『专门覆盖』酒店、餐厅、公寓、出租车这四个细分领域，specializing in 是地道搭配；语\n义指『各有专攻的平台』。\n【竞争词】I) persisting persist in = 执着坚持，对象应是抽象行为而非行业\npersist in hotels and restaurants 在英语里不成立——persist in 后接 doing/努力，描述『在\n酒店里坚持』没有意义，违背 specialize 与 persist 的语义分工。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) professionals\n名词\n【词性槽】by 介词后 + not...but 对比的另一端是 the customer\n【依据】industry professionals = 业内专业人士，与 the customer 形成正反对\nnot by industry __(29)__, but by the very people for whom the service is intended—\nthe customer 句意要求把『打分主体』从过去的『行业内行』转到『顾客本人』；\nindustry professionals 是固定搭配，正好对上 the customer 的反面。\n【竞争词】F) incentives 义为『激励』，无法与 by 搭配做评分主体\nincentives 是抽象名词『激励/动机』，industry incentives 不构成评分主体，与 the customer\n也对不上反义。\nN) spectators spectators 是『观赛观众』，与 hospitality 行业不沾边\nspectators 指体育/演出场合的看客，industry spectators 既非固定搭配，也无法承担『过去给\n业内打分的人』这一语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) forged\n动词\n【词性槽】This has + 空 + a new relationship，完成时态的过去分词\n【依据】forge a new relationship = 缔造一种新关系，固定搭配\nThis has forged a new relationship between buyer and seller.：forge a\nrelationship/alliance/bond 是英语母语者描述『从无到有、费力建立关系』的高频搭配，\n与新评分文化『新关系』的力度匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accountable\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 are + much more + 空\n【依据】accountable = 须对（顾客）担责的，与下文『顾客可向任何人解释决定』完美呼应\nCustomers...can now explain their decision to anyone who is interested. As a result,\nbusinesses are much more accountable：顾客把意见公开 → 商家须对顾客负责，\naccountable 是『对...负责/受问责』的标准词，并直接驱动下句 powerful incentives to\nimprove service 的因果链。\n【竞争词】J) pessimistic pessimistic = 悲观的，与全文积极变革主线矛盾\n全文论述新关系如何促使商家『改善服务』，若填 more pessimistic 则逻辑反转，且\npessimistic 通常修饰人的心态而非商家行为属性。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) incentives\n名词\n【词性槽】creates + 形容词 powerful + 空 + 不定式 to improve\n【依据】powerful incentives to improve service = 改善服务的强大动力\nbusinesses are much more __(31)__, often in very specific ways, which creates\npowerful incentives to improve service.：商家被问责 → 产生强烈的改善动力，\nincentive 与 to do 搭配是英语高频固定结构。\n【竞争词】B) capacity capacity 通常不可数、且义为『能力』而非『动机』\npowerful capacities 几乎不用复数；capacity to do 表『有能力做』，但本句强调的是『动力/\n驱动』，逻辑不对。B 留给第 33 题 websites' capacity to aggregate。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) capacity\n名词\n【词性槽】所有格 the websites' + 空 + to do\n【依据】capacity to aggregate = 汇集（评分）的能力\nthe true power of online reviews lies...in the websites' capacity to aggregate a large\nvolume of ratings.：在线评论的力量不在单条故事，而在网站『汇集海量评分』的能力，\ncapacity to do 表能力恰好。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) overstated\n动词\n【词性槽】cannot be + 过去分词，被动情态结构\n【依据】cannot be overstated = 怎么强调也不为过，固定表达\nThe impact cannot be overstated. 是英语写作中评价某事影响巨大的高频句式，紧接后文\n论述 Businesses that attract top ratings can enjoy rapid growth，正是『影响极大、再\n强调都不为过』。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accountable", "B) capacity", "C) controlled", "D) entail", "E) forged", "F) incentives", "G) occasionally", "H) overstated", "I) persisting", "J) pessimistic", "K) professionals", "L) slash", "M) specializing", "N) spectators", "O) subsequently"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) subsequently\n副词\n【词性槽】and 后并列两个谓语动词 attracted / provide 之间需要副词修饰\n【依据】subsequently = 随后，正好接续 attracted → provide feedback 的时序\nnew customers are attracted by good reviews and subsequently provide yet more\npositive feedback：新顾客先被好评吸引、随后又留下更多好评，形成正反馈循环，\nsubsequently 精准表达这种因果与时序。\n【竞争词】G) occasionally occasionally = 偶尔，与正反馈循环矛盾\n若填 occasionally，则新顾客『偶尔』才反馈，无法支撑前句 rapid growth 的强势成长，逻辑\n相反。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— occasionally 词性对，但逻辑反。副词槽里 G\noccasionally 与 O subsequently 都是候选，主体考生若只看词性，极易误选\noccasionally；防坑要诀——读完后半句 provide yet more positive feedback 后再回头\n判断要时序（subsequently）还是频率（occasionally）。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is best to use an EMV card for international travel.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】出国旅行（international travel）\n【定位】Should you take them up on it? If you travel internationally, the answer is yes.\n你应该接受银行提供的这种升级吗？如果你常出国，答案是肯定的。\n【改写】an EMV card ↔ a chip-and-PIN model\nEMV 卡 = 芯片 + PIN 卡（专有术语等价）\ninternational travel ↔ If you travel internationally\ninternational travel 与 travel internationally 词形微变\nIt is best to use ↔\nShould you take them up on it? If you travel internationally, the answer is yes.\n‘最好做’与‘问 + 肯定回答’句式等值\n【辨邻】 C C 段的核心是 much of the rest of the world uses smarter cards with a\ntechnology called EMV——是技术定义与现状对比，并没有出现‘出国时应使用’这层\n建议。它制造的迷惑是‘EMV 一词在此段最显眼’，但并非源头段。\nWhile American credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to process transactions,\nmuch of the rest of the world uses smarter cards with a technology called EMV", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Personal information on credit and debit cards is increasingly vulnerable to hacking.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】信用卡和借记卡上的个人信息 + 越发易受攻击（increasingly vulnerable）\n【定位】Swipe(刷卡) is the operative word: cards are increasingly vulnerable to attacks when you\nmake purchases in a store. In several recent incidents, hackers have been able to obtain\nmassive information of credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-card numbers using malware,\ni.e. malicious software, inserted secretly into the retailers' point-of-sale system—the\ncheckout registers.\n‘刷卡’是关键词：当你在店里付款时，卡正变得越来越容易被攻击。在最近几起事件中，黑\n客把恶意软件偷偷植入零售商的销售点系统（即收银机），从而批量获取信用卡、借记卡或\n预付卡的卡号。\n【改写】Personal information on credit and debit cards ↔ credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-card numbers\n卡号是个人信息的下位实例\nincreasingly vulnerable to hacking ↔ cards are increasingly vulnerable to attacks\nincreasingly vulnerable 同根近义原样复现\n【辨邻】 A banks, law enforcement and technology companies are all trying to stop a\nnetwork of hackers 与 More than 100 million accounts at Target, Neiman\nMarcus and Michaels stores were affected 的论述焦点是‘反击 + 受害规模’，不是\n陈述句要求的‘信用卡 + 借记卡上个人信息越来越易被黑’这一直接断言。\nbanks, law enforcement and technology companies are all trying to stop a network of hackers who\nare succeeding in stealing account numbers, names, email addresses and other crucial data used in\nidentity theft\nF Each magstripe has three tracks of information 的论述是‘磁条物理结构 + 第二轨\n被攻击’，没有‘越来越脆弱’这层动态判断，也没有 credit + debit 两类卡并列的表述。\nYour vital account information lives on the second track, which hackers try to capture", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The French card companies adopted EMV technology partly because of inefficient telephone service.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】法国卡公司 + 低效的电话服务\n【定位】In France, card companies created EMV in part because the telephone monopoly was so\nmaddeningly inefficient and expensive.\n在法国，卡公司之所以创建了 EMV，部分原因是当时的电话垄断公司极其低效和昂贵。\n【改写】The French card companies ↔ In France, card companies\n形容词形式 vs 介词短语等值\nadopted EMV technology ↔ created EMV\nadopt 与 create 在‘引入新技术’语境下近义\ninefficient telephone service ↔\nthe telephone monopoly was so maddeningly inefficient and expensive\n名词短语 vs 表语描述等值\npartly because ↔ in part because\n因果连接词原样近义复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "While many countries use the smarter EMV cards, the U.S. still clings to its old magstripe technology.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】许多国家用 EMV vs 美国仍守磁条\n【定位】While American credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to process\ntransactions, much of the rest of the world uses smarter cards with a technology called\nEMV (short for Europay, MasterCard, Visa) that employs a chip embedded in the card plus\na customer PIN (personal identification number) to authenticate(验证) every transaction on\nthe spot.\n尽管美国的信用卡仍在用 40 年历史的磁条技术处理交易，但世界其他大多数地区已用上了\n更智能的卡——这种卡采用一种叫做 EMV 的技术，靠卡内嵌入的芯片加上消费者的 PIN 码\n在交易现场验证每一笔交易。\n【改写】many countries use the smarter EMV cards ↔\nmuch of the rest of the world uses smarter cards with a technology called EMV\n‘many countries’ ↔ ‘much of the rest of the world’是数量级同义替换\nthe U.S. still clings to its old magstripe technology ↔\nAmerican credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to process transactions\n‘clings to its old magstripe’ ↔ ‘use the 40-year-old magstripe’动词色彩改写\n【辨邻】 G The historical reason the U.S. has stuck with magstripe, ironically enough, is\nonce superior technology 论述的是‘美国为何坚守磁条’这一历史原因，而非陈述句所\n说的‘很多国家用 EMV 而美国仍用磁条’这一现状对比。‘stuck with magstripe’的词面\n也制造迷惑。\nThe historical reason the U.S. has stuck with magstripe, ironically enough, is once\nsuperior technology\nF That leaves American retailers pretty much alone the world over in relying on\nmagstripe technology to charge purchases—and leaves consumers vulnerable\n——重心在‘让消费者暴露’的后果，没有把 EMV 作为对比的另一极并列出来。\nThat leaves American retailers pretty much alone the world over in relying on magstripe technology\nto charge purchases—and leaves consumers vulnerable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Attempts are being made to prevent hackers from carrying out identity theft.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】正在做出努力阻止黑客实施身份盗窃\n【定位】That's why 2014 is shaping up as a major showdown: banks, law enforcement and technology\ncompanies are all trying to stop a network of hackers who are succeeding in stealing account\nnumbers, names, email addresses and other crucial data used in identity theft.\n这就是为什么 2014 年将是一场大对决：银行、执法机构和科技公司都在合力阻止一个黑客\n网络——这个网络正在成功窃取账户、姓名、邮箱等用于身份盗窃的关键数据。\n【改写】Attempts are being made ↔\nbanks, law enforcement and technology companies are all trying to stop\n被动 ‘Attempts are being made’ 等于多主语主动 ‘are all trying to’\nprevent hackers from carrying out identity theft ↔\nstop a network of hackers who are succeeding in stealing account numbers, names, email\naddresses and other crucial data used in identity theft\nprevent…from doing ↔ stop…who are doing 等值\n【辨邻】 M The card-payment companies MasterCard and Visa are pushing hard for\nchange 的‘push for change’是机制层面的‘推动转换’，未直接陈述‘阻止黑客实施身份\n盗窃’，且不含 identity theft 一词。\nThe card-payment companies MasterCard and Visa are pushing hard for change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Credit cards are much safer to use than debit cards.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】信用卡比借记卡安全得多\n【定位】Keep in mind, too, that credit cards typically have better liability protection than debit cards.\n也请记住，信用卡通常比借记卡有更好的赔付责任保护。\n【改写】Credit cards are much safer to use than debit cards ↔\ncredit cards typically have better liability protection than debit cards\n‘safer’与‘better liability protection’是上位 vs 具体的同义改写\nmuch safer ↔ I would use credit cards over debit cards because of liability issues\n‘much safer’得到原文专家偏好语的支撑\n【辨邻】 B credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-card numbers 把两类卡并列只是为说明‘黑客可同时\n窃取两类卡的卡号’，没有对比 credit 和 debit 的安全性高低。\nhackers have been able to obtain massive information of credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-\ncard numbers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Big banks have been reluctant to switch to more secure technology because of the higher costs involved.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大银行不愿换 + 因为成本更高\n【定位】Why haven't big banks adopted the more secure technology? When it comes to mailing out\nnew credit cards, it's all about relative costs, says David Robertson, who runs the Nilson\nReport, an industry newsletter.\n为什么大银行还没采用更安全的技术？David Robertson（行业通讯 Nilson Report 的负责\n人）说，谈到寄出新信用卡，归根结底是相对成本的问题。\n【改写】Big banks have been reluctant to switch ↔\nWhy haven't big banks adopted the more secure technology?\n‘haven't adopted’与‘reluctant to switch’行为 ↔ 心理同义\nbecause of the higher costs involved ↔ it's all about relative costs\n‘relative costs’对应‘higher costs’的具体落点\nmore secure technology ↔ the more secure technology\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 E Card issuers have so far calculated that absorbing the liability for even big\nhacks like the Target one is still cheaper than replacing all that plastic 是结论性\n总账，不是大银行迟迟不换的直接原因表述；陈述句问的是‘为何不愿换’，E 段答的是\n‘吸收欺诈仍便宜’的衡量结果。\nCard issuers have so far calculated that absorbing the liability for even big hacks like the Target one\nis still cheaper than replacing all that plastic\nJ Retailers and banks stand to benefit from the lower fraud levels of chip-and-\nPIN cards but have been reluctant for years to invest in the new\ninfrastructure(基础设施) needed for the technology 谈的是双方互推投资意愿，与\n陈述句的‘大银行 + 成本 + 不愿换卡’这一单方面因果不对应。\nhave been reluctant for years to invest in the new infrastructure(基础设施) needed for\nthe technology", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The potential liability for retailers using magstripe is far more costly than upgrading their registers.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】零售商用磁条的潜在赔付风险 vs 升级收银机成本\n【定位】The Target and Neiman hacks have also changed the cost calculation: although retailers have\nbeen reluctant to spend the $6.75 billion that Capgemini consultants estimate it will take to\nconvert all their registers to be chip-and-PIN-compatible, the potential liability they now face\nis dramatically greater.\nTarget 和 Neiman 两起入侵事件也改变了成本账：尽管零售商一直不情愿花费 Capgemini\n顾问估算的 67.5 亿美元改造所有收银机以兼容芯片 + PIN，但他们现在面临的潜在赔付风\n险已远远更大。\n【改写】The potential liability for retailers using magstripe ↔\nthe potential liability they now face is dramatically greater\npotential liability 原样复现\nfar more costly than upgrading their registers ↔\n$6.75 billion that Capgemini consultants estimate it will take to convert all their registers to be\nchip-and-PIN-compatible\n‘upgrade registers’对应‘convert all their registers to be chip-and-PIN-compatible’的概括\n等值\n【辨邻】 D A chip-and-PIN card currently costs closer to $3 与 you're in the dollar range 的\n对比是单卡 3 vs 1，与陈述句的‘潜在赔付 vs 升级收银机’不在同一对比维度。\nA chip-and-PIN card currently costs closer to $3\nE Card issuers have so far calculated that absorbing the liability for even big\nhacks like the Target one is still cheaper than replacing all that plastic 是发卡\n方角度的‘吸收赔付便宜’，主体与陈述句的零售商不同，结论方向也相反，正话反说\n陷阱。\nabsorbing the liability for even big hacks like the Target one is still cheaper than replacing all\nthat plastic", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The use of magstripe cards by American retailers leaves consumers exposed to the risks of losing account information.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】美国零售商用磁条 + 让消费者面临账户信息泄露风险\n【定位】That leaves American retailers pretty much alone the world over in relying on magstripe\ntechnology to charge purchases—and leaves consumers vulnerable.\n这让美国零售商在全世界范围内几乎成了仍然依赖磁条技术收款的孤例，也让消费者暴露在\n风险之中。\n【改写】The use of magstripe cards by American retailers ↔\nAmerican retailers pretty much alone the world over in relying on magstripe technology to\ncharge purchases\n名词化短语 vs 动词化原文等值\nleaves consumers exposed to the risks of losing account information ↔\nleaves consumers vulnerable\n‘exposed to the risks of losing account information’ ↔ ‘vulnerable + 下文账户信息被捕’上位\nvs 具体的对应\n【辨邻】 B hackers have been able to obtain massive information of credit-debit-(借记) or\nprepaid-card numbers using malware 论述的是入侵路径，主语是 hackers；陈述\n句要求主语是 American retailers 的‘使用磁条’行为导致‘消费者暴露’，方向不一致。\nhackers have been able to obtain massive information of credit-debit-(借记) or prepaid-card\nnumbers using malware, i.e. malicious software, inserted secretly into the retailers' point-of-\nsale system\nC While American credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to\nprocess transactions 仅停在现状对比，没有把后果落到‘消费者暴露于账户信息丢\n失’上。\nWhile American credit cards use the 40-year-old magstripe technology to process transactions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Consumers will be a driving force behind the conversion from magstripe to EMV technology.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】消费者将成为从磁条转向 EMV 的推动力\n【定位】That's why it may be up to consumers to move the needle on chip and PIN.\n这就是为什么推动芯片 + PIN 普及的关键可能要落在消费者身上。\n【改写】Consumers will be a driving force ↔\nit may be up to consumers to move the needle on chip and PIN\n‘be up to X’ ↔ ‘X will be a driving force’同义改写\nthe conversion from magstripe to EMV technology ↔ chip and PIN\n‘from magstripe to EMV’ ↔ ‘chip and PIN’专有术语等价\nConsumers ↔\nWhen you get the consumer into a position of worry and inconvenience, that's where the\nrubber hits the road\n进一步佐证：原文以‘消费者陷入担忧时才会真正发生’再次锁定消费者为关键推动者\n【辨邻】 M The card-payment companies MasterCard and Visa are pushing hard for\nchange 把推动者定为卡公司，与陈述句的‘消费者’主体不一致。\nThe card-payment companies MasterCard and Visa are pushing hard for change\nK JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon recently expressed his willingness to do so\n把率先动作的主体定为银行 CEO，与陈述句的‘消费者’主体不一致。\nJPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon recently expressed his willingness to do so", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "How do pessimists interpret the U.S. trade deficit in June?", "options": ["A) It reflects Americans' preference for imported goods.", "B) It signifies a change in American economic structure.", "C) It could lead to slower growth of the national economy.", "D) It is the result of America's growing focus on domestic market."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How do pessimists interpret the U.S. trade deficit in June? 问的是\"悲观派如何解读\"，\n指向原文某一处具体陈述。\n【拆句】How do pessimists interpret the U.S. trade deficit in June? 是一个 how 引导的特殊疑\n问句，主语 pessimists，谓语 interpret，宾语 the U.S. trade deficit in June。结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Should such imbalances continue, pessimists say, they could contribute to slower growth.\n悲观派说，如果这种（贸易）失衡持续下去，它们可能会导致经济增长放缓。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt reflects Americans' preference for imported goods. 把贸易逆差归因于美国人\"偏\n好进口商品\"，但 pessimists say 那句完全没谈消费者偏好，纯是\"宏观增速被拖累\"。\nB) 过度推断\nIt signifies a change in American economic structure. 把贸易逆差升级为\"经济结构\n变化\"的征兆，P2 只提到 GDP 数字变小，并未谈结构层面的转变。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthey could contribute to slower growth ↔\nIt could lead to slower growth of the national economy.\nIt could lead to slower growth of the national economy. 与预判\"导致经济增长放\n缓\"逐词对应，情态动词 could 也与原文 could contribute to 一致，不夸大不弱化。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is the result of America's growing focus on domestic market. 把贸易逆差解释\n成\"美国越来越聚焦国内市场\"，与 Imports, which soared to $200.3 billion 暗示的\"进\n口飙升\"完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say about the trade data of the past two years?", "options": ["A) It indicates that economic activities in the U.S. have increased.", "B) It signals decreasing domestic demand for goods and services.", "C) It reflects the fluctuations in the international market.", "D) It shows that U.S. economy is slipping further into recession."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the trade data of the past two years? 指向作者就某\n一具体话题的明确陈述，定位即可解。\n【拆句】What does the author say about the trade data of the past two years? 是 what 引导\n的特殊疑问句，问的是 say about 的内容；about 介词短语限定话题范围。结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】But there's another way of looking at the trade data. Over the past two years, the figures on\nimports and exports seem not to signal a double-dip recession—a renewed decline in the\nbroad level of economic activity in the United States—but an economic expansion.\n但还有另一种看待贸易数据的方式。过去两年里，进出口数据似乎并不预示二次衰退（即美\n国整体经济活动的再次下降），反而预示经济扩张。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbut an economic expansion ↔\nIt indicates that economic activities in the U.S. have increased.\n预判要的就是\"经济在扩张\"，It indicates that economic activities in the U.S. have\nincreased. 用现在完成时\"have increased\"复述\"扩张\"，与作者立场逐字对齐。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt signals decreasing domestic demand for goods and services. 把贸易数据解读\n成\"国内需求下降的信号\"，P3 只谈\"扩张/二次衰退\"，未把数据与国内需求挂钩。\nC) 无中生有\nIt reflects the fluctuations in the international market. 引入\"国际市场波动\"，P3 既\n未提波动、也未对国际市场作此描述。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt shows that U.S. economy is slipping further into recession. 说\"美国经济进一步陷\n入衰退\"，恰恰是 not to signal a double-dip recession 否定的内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Who particularly benefits from the rising volume of trade?", "options": ["A) People who have expertise in international trade.", "B) Consumers who favor imported goods and services.", "C) Producers of agricultural goods and raw materials.", "D) Retailers dealing in foreign goods and services."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Who particularly benefits from the rising volume of trade? 问的是\"具体哪类人\"，属典\n型的指人细节题。\n【拆句】Who particularly benefits from the rising volume of trade? 是 who 引导的特殊疑问\n句，benefits 是不及物动词，from 引导动作来源。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The rising tide of trade is also good news for people who work in trade-sensitive businesses,\nespecially those that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—\nagricultural goods, mining, metals, oil.\n贸易的上升势头对在贸易敏感行业工作的人也是好消息，尤其是那些生产\"由全球需求决定\n价格的大宗商品\"的从业者——农产品、采矿、金属、石油。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nPeople who have expertise in international trade. 把受益群体限定为\"国际贸易专\n才\"，P4 谈的是 shipping/trucking/物流/大宗商品从业者，没有\"专才\"这一限定。\nB) 张冠李戴\nConsumers who favor imported goods and services. 把受益方说成\"偏好进口商品的\n消费者\"，P4 通篇谈的是企业与生产者，不是消费者。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthose that produce commodities for which global demand sets the price—agricultural goods,\nmining, metals, oil\n↔ Producers of agricultural goods and raw materials.\nProducers of agricultural goods and raw materials. 把原文四项列举（农产品+矿、\n金属、油）精准归并为 agricultural goods + raw materials，与预判一一对应。\nD) 无中生有\nRetailers dealing in foreign goods and services. 引入\"零售商\"，P4 完全没有\nretailer 这一身份，也没把\"零售\"与受益挂钩。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is one of the challenges facing the American economy?", "options": ["A) Decreasing productivity.", "B) Competition from overseas.", "C) People's reluctance to spend.", "D) Slack trade activities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the challenges facing the American economy? 指向原文具体列举的某\n一项挑战，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】What is one of the challenges facing the American economy? 是 what 引导的特殊疑\n问句；facing the American economy 是现在分词短语作 challenges 的后置定语。结构清\n晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】One great challenge for the U.S. economy is slack domestic consumer demand. Americans\nare paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully.\n美国经济的一大挑战是国内消费需求疲软。美国人在还债、增加储蓄、更谨慎地消费。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nDecreasing productivity. 引入\"生产率下降\"，P6 完全未涉及 productivity 这一概念。\nB) 无中生有\nCompetition from overseas. 引入\"海外竞争\"，P6 只谈\"国内消费疲软\"以及\"如何抓住\n全球需求\"，没把竞争作为挑战提出来。\nC) ✓ 正确\nslack domestic consumer demand ↔ People's reluctance to spend.\nAmericans are paying down debt, saving more, and spending more carefully. ↔\nPeople's reluctance to spend.\nPeople's reluctance to spend. 是对 slack domestic consumer demand 与紧随其后\n的三项行为列举的最准确归纳，无加无减。\nD) 偷换概念\nSlack trade activities. 把 P6 的 slack 错挂到 trade 上，但 P6 中 slack 修饰的对象是\ndomestic consumer demand，而非 trade。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the author's advice to U.S. companies and individuals?", "options": ["A) To import more cheap goods from developing countries.", "B) To increase their market share overseas.", "C) To be alert to fluctuations in foreign markets.", "D) To move their companies to where labor is cheaper."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题）\n【判型】What is the author's advice to U.S. companies and individuals? 问作者面向全部受众的\n建议，是全文论点的落地表述，需要把全文走向串起来作答。\n【拆句】What is the author's advice to U.S. companies and individuals? 是 what 引导的特殊疑\n问句；to U.S. companies and individuals 是介词短语，指明建议对象。结构平铺直叙，\n无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTo import more cheap goods from developing countries. 把建议说成\"从发展中\n国家进口廉价商品\"，P6/P7 谈的是\"出口\"和\"参与外国市场\"，与\"进口廉价商品\"方向\n相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\na strategy to export more, or to get more involved in foreign markets, or to play a role in global\ntrade\n↔ To increase their market share overseas.\nthe best way to do that is through trade ↔ To increase their market share overseas.\nTo increase their market share overseas. 把 P6 末句的\"借贸易抓全球需求\"与 P7\n的\"出口/参与外国市场/全球贸易\"统一收口为\"在海外扩大市场份额\"，覆盖全部受众。\nC) 无中生有\nTo be alert to fluctuations in foreign markets. 引入\"警惕外国市场波动\"的建议，\nP6/P7 通篇没有 fluctuation 这一议题。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo move their companies to where labor is cheaper. 把建议说成\"把公司搬到劳动力\n更便宜的地方\"，Unless you want to pick up and move to India, or Brazil, or China\n恰恰是把\"搬过去\"当成\"除非\"前提，作者真正建议是\"通过贸易\"代替搬迁。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think of UK universities in terms of commercialisation?", "options": ["A) They still have a place among the world leaders.", "B) They have lost their leading position in many ways.", "C) They do not regard it as their responsibility.", "D) They fail to convert knowledge into money."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of UK universities in terms of commercialisation 是\"作者\n对某具体事物的具体看法\"，限定在 commercialisation 这一维度，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】Q51 主干是 What does the author think of X 的标准疑问句，in terms of\ncommercialisation 是介词短语作状语，把范围限定到\"商业化\"这一维度上；句法平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】However, we do have to challenge the unthinking complaint that the sector does not do\nenough in taking ideas to market. The most recent comparative data on the performance\nof universities and research institutions in Australia, Canada, USA and UK shows\nthat, from a relatively weak starting position, the UK now leads on many indicators of\ncommercialisation activity.\n然而，我们确实需要反驳那种不假思索的抱怨——说英国高校在把创意推向市场方面做得不\n够。最新的对比数据显示，英国从相对薄弱的起点出发，如今在商业化活动的许多指标上处\n于领先地位。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe UK now leads on many indicators of commercialisation activity ↔\nstill have a place among the world leaders\n预判的\"英国大学仍处世界领先\"与 still have a place among the world leaders 同义，\n是定位句的标准同义改写。\nB) 与原文相反\nhave lost their leading position in many ways 与定位句立场完全相反——原文是\nnow leads，B 说的是 lost。\nC) 无中生有\ndo not regard it as their responsibility 谈的是\"是否当作责任\"，而原文谈的是\"做得怎\n么样\"，文中找不到对应表述。\nD) 张冠李戴\nfail to convert knowledge into money 与 translating new knowledge into new\nproducts and services 表面相似，但 P1 是被作者反驳的\"批评\"，不是作者本人的立\n场；作者的立场在 P3。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say about the national data on UK universities' performance in commercialisation?", "options": ["A) It masks the fatal weaknesses of government policy.", "B) It does not reflect the differences among universities.", "C) It does not rank UK universities in a scientific way.", "D) It indicates their ineffective use of government resources."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the national data 把焦点锁在\"作者对 national data\n说了什么\"，属定位+转述型细节题。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What does the author say about X；UK universities' performance in\ncommercialisation 是 X 的定语链，用所有格 + in 短语层层限定，意为\"英国高校在商业化\n方面的表现\"。\n【定位】But national data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities.\n但国家层面的数据掩盖了各高校在表现上巨大的差异。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nmasks the fatal weaknesses of government policy 把 masks 的宾语偷换为\"政府政\n策的致命弱点\"，原文 masks 的真实宾语是 the very large variation in the\nperformance of individual universities。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnational data masks the very large variation in the performance of individual universities ↔\ndoes not reflect the differences among universities\n预判\"掩盖校际差异\"与 does not reflect the differences among universities 完全等\n值，B 即正确项。\nC) 无中生有\ndoes not rank UK universities in a scientific way 把 P4 转折句重新解读为\"排名方式\n不科学\"，文中无 rank/scientific 任何对应表述。\nD) 过度推断\nindicates their ineffective use of government resources 谈的是\"资源使用是否有\n效\"，原文 P4 只说数据掩盖了差异，未对资源使用效率作判断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "We can infer from Paragraph 5 that \"policy interventions\"(Line 1, Para. 4) refers to____.", "options": ["A) Concentration of resources in a limited number of universities", "B) Compulsory cooperation between universities and industries", "C) Government aid to non-research-oriented universities", "D) Fair distribution of funding for universities and research institutions"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】We can infer from Paragraph 5 出现 infer，要求由第 5 段事实跨一步推出 policy\ninterventions 的所指。\n【拆句】Q53 是带宾语从句的推断陈述句；refers to ____ 句末留空，要求填入 policy\ninterventions 的所指；插入语 (Line 1, Para. 4) 仅作定位标注，不参与句法。\n【定位】In the UK, research is concentrated: less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research\nfunding. These same universities are also the institutions producing the greatest share of PhD\ngraduates, science citations, patents and licence income. The effect of policies generating\nlong-term resource concentration has also created a distinctive set of universities which are\nresearch-led and commercially active.\n在英国，研究是集中的：不到 25% 的大学拿走 75% 的研究经费。这些大学同时也产出最大\n份额的博士、科学引用、专利和许可收入。长期产生资源集中的政策效应，还造就了一批以\n研究为主导、商业活跃的特色大学。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nlong-term resource concentration ↔\nconcentration of resources in a limited number of universities\n预判\"资源集中到少数大学\"与 concentration of resources in a limited number of\nuniversities 完全等值。\nB) 无中生有\ncompulsory cooperation between universities and industries 谈\"强制校企合作\"，\n第 5 段未提任何 cooperation 与 industry。\nC) 与原文相反\ngovernment aid to non-research-oriented universities 把资助对象说成\"非研究型大\n学\"，与 less than 25% of universities receive 75% of the research funding 中真正\n受惠的研究型主导大学正好相反。\nD) 与原文相反\nfair distribution of funding for universities and research institutions 强调\"公平分\n配\"，与 research is concentrated 的集中现状方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author suggest research-led universities do?", "options": ["A) Fully utilise their research to benefit all sectors of society.", "B) Generously share their facilities with those short of funds.", "C) Publicise their research to win international recognition.", "D) Spread their influence among top research institutions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest research-led universities do 把焦点锁在\"对某具体对象\n的具体建议\"，属细节题。\n【拆句】Q54 是 suggest + 宾语 + 省略 to 的不定式结构（虚拟语气），意为\"建议研究型大学做什\n么\"；动词 do 后留空，由选项补足。\n【定位】The core objective for universities which are research-led must be to maximise the impact of\ntheir research efforts. These universities should be generating the widest range of social,\neconomic and environmental benefits.\n以研究为主导的大学，其核心目标必须是把研究努力的影响最大化。这些大学应当产出最广\n泛的社会、经济与环境效益。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nshould be generating the widest range of social, economic and environmental benefits ↔\nFully utilise their research to benefit all sectors of society\n预判\"充分利用研究、惠及社会各部门\"与 Fully utilise their research to benefit all\nsectors of society 完全等值。\nB) 偷换概念\nGenerously share their facilities with those short of funds 把 share their expertise\n偷换为\"分享设施\"，对象也被偷换为\"经费不足者\"。\nC) 无中生有\nPublicise their research to win international recognition 谈\"宣传研究、争国际声\n誉\"，文中无 international recognition 任何对应表述。\nD) 以偏概全\nSpread their influence among top research institutions 把影响对象从\"全社会\"窄化\n为\"顶尖研究机构\"，与 the widest range of social, economic and environmental\nbenefits 的覆盖面不符。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How can the university sector play a key role in the UK's economic growth?", "options": ["A) By establishing more regional technology transfer offices.", "B) By asking the government to invest in technology transfer research.", "C) By promoting technology transfer and graduate school education.", "D) By increasing the efficiency of technology transfer agencies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How can the university sector play a key role 询问\"通过什么方式实现作用\"，需在文末\n对策句中找具体手段。\n【拆句】Q55 是 How can 引导的疑问句；play a key role in 是固定搭配\"在……中发挥关键作用\"，\n介词 in 后跟领域名词，需把这一搭配视为整体理解。\n【定位】If there was a greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions and a\nsimultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools, universities\ncould, and should, play a key role in positioning the U.K. for the next growth cycle.\n如果各区域内的技术转移办公室能更好地协调、同时对研究生院的规模与职能进行投入，大\n学就能够、也应当在英国驶向下一轮增长周期的过程中发挥关键作用。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nestablishing more regional technology transfer offices 把 greater coordination of\ntechnology transfer offices 偷换为\"新建更多办公室\"，coordination（协作）与\nestablishing more（增设）方向不同。\nB) 偷换概念\nasking the government to invest in technology transfer research 把投资对象从 the\nscale and functions of our graduate schools 偷换为\"技术转移研究\"；且\"请政府投\n资\"也是文中没有的主体。\nC) ✓ 正确\na greater coordination of technology transfer offices within regions ↔\npromoting technology transfer\na simultaneous investment in the scale and functions of our graduate schools ↔\ngraduate school education\npromoting technology transfer and graduate school education 用 and 把两条手段\n并列拎出，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 偷换概念\nincreasing the efficiency of technology transfer agencies 谈\"提高技术转移机构效\n率\"，原文 P8 谈的是 coordination（协调），未涉及 efficiency 评价。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
